/* BTEC Business Unit 2 — Pearson past papers (text extracted from the published papers; generated by training-data/btec/level-3/business/build). */
window.BIZ_PAPERS = [{"id":"U2-1706","unit":2,"key":"2017-06","series":"June 2017","title":"RokU — augmented reality game launch","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign for the launch of RokU’s new augmented reality game. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include:\n• an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools\n• target market\n• size, structure and trends\n• competition\n• evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for RokU, which should include an appropriate:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owner of RokU.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A: Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The Market for Mobile Apps including Games A mobile application, most commonly referred to as an app, is a type of application software designed to run on a mobile device, such as a smartphone or tablet. People of all ages are now using these devices for work and leisure purposes. With the increasing availability and reliability of public Wi-Fi and fast mobile data, more people are using mobile devices to access online services. In 2016, 46% of smartphone users played games on a daily basis. Many games use a ‘freemium’ business model. This refers to giving a basic service for free and then charging users for access to additional features."},{"t":"p","x":"The strength of the UK’s mobile app development market is widely acknowledged. The UK currently has approximately 8 000 app development businesses that employ roughly 400 000 people. These businesses make money both in the UK and in export markets and are currently the largest provider of app development services outside the USA. It is estimated that annual sales from mobile apps, including games, will exceed £4 billion in 2016, and that revenues will reach £31 billion by 2025."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete ALL activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign for the launch of RokU’s new augmented reality game."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include:","an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools","target market","size, structure and trends","competition","evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for RokU, which should include an appropriate:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owner of RokU."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B: Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"Getting Started Steve is very good at developing mobile games. While at university he created a number of these programmes, which he sold online. Steve has set up a business that is focused entirely on creating games and apps. He has enjoyed some success so far and now wishes to expand the business."},{"t":"p","x":"RokU Developments Steve decided to create a brand for his business and chose the name RokU to reflect his taste in music."},{"t":"p","x":"In order to expand the business he has recruited a number of other recent university graduates to work for him."},{"t":"p","x":"Steve and his team are developing a new augmented reality game. The game is based around a fantasy role-play scenario in which players take on the role of different characters. The players collect virtual rewards for completing different quests. Steve is not sure which pricing strategy to use for the new game. The strategies he could use are:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1 a ‘freemium’ approach where users are able to download the game for free but must pay for additional features OR 2 charging users a one-off fee to download a copy of the game. Steve has set an initial marketing budget of £30 000 for the next 12 months, but he is hoping to use innovative techniques to make the money go as far as possible."},{"t":"p","x":"7 What is ‘freemium’ pricing?"},{"t":"p","x":"Freemium pricing is where companies give the public access to a product free of charge but offer them the opportunity to pay to access additional features or content. For example, some games may charge users to unlock additional characters or extra levels. What is augmented reality?"},{"t":"p","x":"Augmented reality is where games make use of the cameras contained in a mobile device in order to transform players’ surroundings into a live gaming environment. For example, this may involve making characters appear in front of the user on the screen of their device. Some more sophisticated games also use the GPS technology in mobile devices to incorporate nearby landmarks or to allow players to compete with other nearby gamers."},{"t":"p","x":"8 Complete your work in the space provided."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":null}},{"id":"U2-1801","unit":2,"key":"2018-01","series":"January 2018","title":"Folding bicycles","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign to increase the sales of folding bicycles. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include:\n• an analysis of your research using appropriate tools\n• target market\n• size, structure and trends\n• competition\n• evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for The Bicycle Shop which should include an appropriate:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate format for the owners of The Bicycle Shop.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A — independent research brief"},{"t":"p","x":"In this series Part A was a short brief: learners were asked to research the market for the business named in Part B independently (competitors, market size, share and structure, target market, trends, external influences and an example campaign), considering the reliability and validity of the data they collected, and to prepare up to six sides of A4 notes to take into the 3-hour Part B session."},{"t":"p","x":"The Part B scenario and the two activities (rationale and campaign plan) follow. Use the Part B information, and for practice research the market yourself, or try the more recent series that include a ready-made research pack."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign to increase the sales of folding bicycles. This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include:","an analysis of your research using appropriate tools","target market","size, structure and trends","competition","evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for The Bicycle Shop which should include an appropriate:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate format for the owners of The Bicycle Shop."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK Bicycle Market The number of bicycles sold globally in 2015 was the same as it was in 2005. During the same period:"},{"t":"li","x":["the number of bicycles sold in the UK decreased by around 10%","spending on bicycles in the UK increased by over 40%."]},{"t":"p","x":"In 2015 cycling contributed £2.9bn to the UK economy:"},{"t":"li","x":["85% was from the sale of bicycles and cycling accessories","15% was from related services such as repairs."]},{"t":"p","x":"In the UK 40% of all bicycle sales are through two retail chains, Halfords and Evans. There is a significant concentration of bicycle sales in UK ‘urban cycling hot spots’ including London, Leeds and Birmingham. In these cities the most popular cycling products are folding bicycles such as those produced by premium brands e.g. Brompton Cycles. Approximately 3.6 million bicycles were imported into the UK in 2015, which was similar to the annual average for the previous 10 years. Many of these imported bicycles were manufactured in Taiwan. In 2014 Taiwanese manufacturers sold 588,000 bicycles to UK retailers, with an approximate average retail value of £179 each. The national governing body for cycle sport in the UK, British Cycling, reported that applications for membership increased in 2015. There was also growing interest in different types of competitive cycling."},{"t":"p","x":"6 The Bicycle Shop The Bicycle Shop is a medium-sized independent retail store. The business currently focuses mainly on sales of bicycles and accessories aimed at commuters. The Bicycle Shop’s customers include a mixture of affluent professionals and price- conscious customers. Sales revenues have been growing steadily from month to month, but the owners of the business believe that there is potential to further increase revenue by selling more from their existing range of products."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners wish to launch a marketing campaign that will focus on one particular segment of the market. They would like to attract more customers to the store and increase the probability of them making a purchase. The campaign should run for 12 to 16 weeks."},{"t":"p","x":"Promoting Existing Products The owners of The Bicycle Shop set it up to capitalise on the popularity of cycling among urban commuters. Many of their existing customers buy bicycles that can be folded for storage while on a train or at work."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners would like to focus on one segment of the market to encourage more people to purchase folding bicycles from them. The campaign will focus on attracting a greater number of:"},{"t":"tbl","x":["EITHER","1. affluent professionals","OR","2. price-conscious customers.","The marketing budget for the campaign is £10,000.","What is commuting by bicycle?"]},{"t":"p","x":"This is when a person regularly travels some or all of the distance from home to work by using a bicycle. Some commuters use a bicycle to travel the whole distance while others will use a bicycle alongside forms of public transport such as a train."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":null}},{"id":"U2-1806","unit":2,"key":"2018-06","series":"June 2018","title":"Wax Perfection — record shop","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign to increase awareness of Wax Perfection’s existing record shop. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include:\n  – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools\n  – target market\n  – size, structure and trends\n  – competition\n• evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Wax Perfection. The plan should include:\n• a marketing mix\n• a marketing message\n• selection of media\n• a campaign budget\n• a timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Wax Perfection.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A — independent research brief"},{"t":"p","x":"In this series Part A was a short brief: learners were asked to research the market for the business named in Part B independently (competitors, market size, share and structure, target market, trends, external influences and an example campaign), considering the reliability and validity of the data they collected, and to prepare up to six sides of A4 notes to take into the 3-hour Part B session."},{"t":"p","x":"The Part B scenario and the two activities (rationale and campaign plan) follow. Use the Part B information, and for practice research the market yourself, or try the more recent series that include a ready-made research pack."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign to increase awareness of Wax Perfection’s existing record shop."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include: - an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools - target market - size, structure and trends - competition","evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Wax Perfection."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["a marketing mix","a marketing message","selection of media","a campaign budget","a timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Wax Perfection."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The Vinyl Album Revival Sales of vinyl records passed one million units a year in 2014 for the first time since 1996. The average price for a vinyl album is £20, considerably more than the price of the same album as a digital download. Special edition vinyl albums featuring extra items such as posters or codes to download additional songs can cost significantly more. When Pink Floyd’s ‘The Wall’ was reissued as a double vinyl album with a poster and a download code it sold for £38."},{"t":"p","x":"The market for vinyl albums in the UK was worth around £5m in 2010. By 2015 the value of the market had increased to around £20m."},{"t":"p","x":"The main purchasers of vinyl albums tend to be men aged over 35, many of whom are collectors."},{"t":"p","x":"One of the reasons for the popularity of vinyl records is the annual ‘Record Store Day’ organised by the music industry to promote sales. Many artists produce exclusive limited edition records that are sold only in smaller records shops on that day. In the UK, vinyl albums account for less than 6% of album sales, although it is the fastest growing segment of the market. In 2014, Pink Floyd’s ‘The Endless River’ became the fastest-selling vinyl album since 1996, despite selling only 6 000 copies during that year. During the same year One Direction had their tracks streamed on Spotify over a billion times."},{"t":"p","x":"7 Wax Perfection Wax Perfection is a record shop in Liverpool. It sells mainly new vinyl albums as well as: - new albums on other formats such as CD, USB and cassette - second-hand albums on a range of formats - related goods such as t-shirts, posters, record players, vinyl record cleaning products and toys."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Wax Perfection organise regular signings by bands and arrange short acoustic performances by artists. These events attract a large number of potential customers to the shop, many of whom have never visited it before. Wax Perfection also takes part in the annual Record Store Day, selling exclusive records produced by a range of artists."},{"t":"p","x":"Boosting Sales Wax Perfection operates a Record of the Month Club. Subscribers pay £35 per month to receive a vinyl album selected by the shop owners. This will always be accompanied by promotional items from the artists involved, such as a bonus CD of exclusive songs, signed photographs of the artists and other items."},{"t":"p","x":"The shop owners currently want to attract more customers to their existing shop. Wax Perfection has experienced staff who are all experts in different types of music. They can advise customers about which records to buy and how to get the most out of their stereo equipment and headphones."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of the business are currently considering focusing their campaign around one form of promotion:"},{"t":"tbl","x":["EITHER","1 Print adverts in specialist music press","OR","2 Distributing leaflets outside music venues after gigs.","The marketing budget for the campaign is £8 000.","What is Record Store Day?"]},{"t":"p","x":"On the third Saturday of April each year, independent record stores around the world host an event to celebrate recorded music. Tens of thousands of records are sold on this day each year, many of them in limited editions of several hundred copies."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":null}},{"id":"U2-1901","unit":2,"key":"2019-01","series":"January 2019","title":"Fantastical Gaming — tabletop games","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign to promote a new tabletop game. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include:\n  – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools\n  – target market\n  – size, structure and trends\n  – competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for the tabletop game manufacturer Fantastical Gaming. This should include an appropriate:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• expenditure budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Fantastical Gaming.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for Fantastical Gaming, a UK-based tabletop game manufacturer."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to increase awareness of their brand and attract more customers. You are required to independently research and analyse the market for tabletop gaming. Your research should include primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences."]},{"t":"p","x":"You should research at least one marketing campaign related to the market for tabletop gaming."},{"t":"p","x":"You may wish to consider researching weekly/monthly marketing journals, marketing and/or advertising agency websites, business consultancies and more general business sources."},{"t":"p","x":"Your research should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["costs","timescale","media used","message communicated","marketing mix","appropriateness of the campaign."]},{"t":"p","x":"You should take into consideration the validity and reliability of the data that you collect. You do not have to produce the promotional materials."},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of six sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for the marketing campaign to promote a new tabletop game. This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include: - an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools - target market - size, structure and trends - competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for the tabletop game manufacturer Fantastical Gaming. This should include an appropriate:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","expenditure budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Fantastical Gaming."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The growth of the market for tabletop games The market for tabletop games is growing rapidly. It is estimated to be worth over $1 billion globally. The UK market alone is estimated to have grown by 20% in 2016 and this growth is forecast to continue."},{"t":"p","x":"These games tend to have a product life cycle of around eight years, although this is expected to increase to 10 years over the next decade. A significant reason for this is the release of ‘expansion packs’ . These offer additional challenges or new characters for existing games."},{"t":"p","x":"Many popular games, for example ‘The 7th Continent’ , started life on crowdfunding platforms such as Kickstarter. This approach can help to build enthusiasm for the project prior to launch, as well as providing the capital needed to fund initial production. Other games, such as ‘Settlers of Catan’ , have found success in the mass market after initially being aimed at a smaller niche."},{"t":"p","x":"The number of games on the market is expected to grow over the next decade. It is expected that there will be an increase in the availability of expansion packs, add-ons and alternative versions of existing games. For example, a version of ‘Settlers of Catan’ has now been successfully launched as a card game."},{"t":"p","x":"Capital obtained from crowdfunding is likely to be a significant driver of growth in this market."},{"t":"p","x":"Another key driver of growth in this market is the increasing availability of these games through physical retail channels. In addition to specialist outlets such as comic book shops, these games can now be bought from an increasing number of supermarkets and discount stores."},{"t":"p","x":"One opportunity to promote these products is at gaming conventions. Events such as ‘Gen Con’ in America and ‘Spiel’ in Germany can each attract over 100 000 people. In 2015, the Spiel convention hosted the largest ever game of ‘Settlers of Catan’ , featuring over 1 000 players."},{"t":"p","x":"The rise in popularity of tabletop gaming has followed the rise in popularity of ‘nerd culture’ . Programmes such as ‘The Big Bang Theory’ and an increase in film adaptation of comic books, such as ‘The Avengers’ , have led to a growing demand for products with science-fiction (sci-fi) and fantasy themes."},{"t":"p","x":"6 Fantastical Gaming Fantastical Gaming is a British manufacturer of tabletop games with sci-fi and fantasy themes. The company makes and sells board games and card games. The owners of Fantastical Gaming currently sell their products at conventions and online through their shop on the Amazon website."},{"t":"p","x":"Taking advantage of a growing market The owners of Fantastical Gaming have purchased new equipment to increase their total production of existing products and to extend their product range. They have allocated a marketing budget to increase demand for their products. Fantastical Gaming is focusing its marketing message on attracting affluent professionals aged 20 to 40. Most promotional activity to date has been through posts on social media and handing out leaflets at UK events."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Fantastical Gaming would like to focus on one of the following distribution channels:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1. UK retail stores selling their products OR 2. Promoting their products at an international convention such as Spiel in Germany. The marketing budget for the campaign is £45 000. The campaign should last up to one year."},{"t":"p","x":"What is tabletop gaming?"},{"t":"p","x":"This can include dice games, board games and card games. These games normally require a flat surface such as a table to play on."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a convention?"},{"t":"p","x":"An event where the players of games and the makers of games get together. Attendees often arrive dressed as characters from their favourite games. Sellers of new and rare collectible gaming items will attend these events. Game designers and players will give talks on particular aspects of gaming."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":"six"}},{"id":"U2-1906","unit":2,"key":"2019-06","series":"June 2019","title":"Starlight Classics — pop-up cinema","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Starlight Classics to promote its summer pop-up cinema events. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include:\n• an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools\n• target market\n• size, structure and trends\n• competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for Starlight Classics. The plan should include:\n• a marketing mix\n• a marketing message\n• a selection of media\n• a campaign budget\n• a timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Starlight Classics.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A — independent research brief"},{"t":"p","x":"In this series Part A was a short brief: learners were asked to research the market for the business named in Part B independently (competitors, market size, share and structure, target market, trends, external influences and an example campaign), considering the reliability and validity of the data they collected, and to prepare up to six sides of A4 notes to take into the 3-hour Part B session."},{"t":"p","x":"The Part B scenario and the two activities (rationale and campaign plan) follow. Use the Part B information, and for practice research the market yourself, or try the more recent series that include a ready-made research pack."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Starlight Classics to promote its summer pop-up cinema events."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include:","an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools","target market","size, structure and trends","competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for your marketing campaign for Starlight Classics."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["a marketing mix","a marketing message","a selection of media","a campaign budget","a timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Starlight Classics."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The cinema industry in the UK Cinemas in the UK generate revenues of over £2bn a year, a figure that grew by 2.7% between 2013 and 2018."},{"t":"p","x":"Revenues are increasing, but the number of customers is declining due to competition from streaming services such as Netflix. Traditional cinema chains increase their revenue through the promotion of value added services such as 3D or IMAX screenings. However, a number of smaller businesses have taken a different approach to adding value to the cinema experience."},{"t":"p","x":"Open-air cinema screenings during the summer months have steadily grown in popularity in the UK. Events such as the Channel 4 sponsored summer screenings at Somerset House in London are relatively well known, and other more specialised events also exist. For example, screenings in unusual locations such as a former prison in Shrewsbury. Other events have themes based on the films with staff and audience dressed as characters from ‘cult classics’ such as ‘Back to the Future’ . Over 60 000 people a year visit the outdoor screenings at London’s Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew. This venue charges around £10 to sit on the grass at the screenings. Customers pay extra for the use of seats and blankets. Premium packages, which can include drinks served by waiting staff and a gourmet picnic, cost hundreds of pounds. The exact cost depends on what the customer orders."},{"t":"p","x":"One of the largest organisers of pop-up cinema events in the UK, Luna Cinema, began trading in 2012. Since then it has regularly held screenings in a range of venues such as swimming pools, castles and on rooftops. It has experienced a growth in customer numbers of around 20% each year."},{"t":"p","x":"The main customers for pop-up cinema events tend to be:"},{"t":"li","x":["film enthusiasts aged 18 to 45","families with children","businesses that want to reward staff or entertain clients."]},{"t":"p","x":"Starlight Classics Starlight Classics runs pop-up cinema events throughout the UK. The business has organised screenings of cult classic films in venues including sports grounds, city parks and old industrial sites."},{"t":"p","x":"The business promotes its screenings as events that offer customers a unique experience. The customer experience may involve staff and audience dressing as characters from the film. There may also be games and competitions for the audience while they wait for screenings to start. Food and drink inspired by the film being shown can be purchased. Customers are offered a range of pricing options."},{"t":"li","x":["Entrance tickets cost £10 per person.","The hire of a blanket for use on the ground costs an extra £5 per person.","Food and drinks are not included in the ticket price but are available at an extra cost .","Premium packages cost between £150 and £500 per person."]},{"t":"p","x":"6 Maximising revenues Customers purchase tickets for Starlight Classics events online. This allows the business to build a customer database. Starlight Classics also operates a Facebook group where it asks for ideas about future screenings."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Starlight Classics want to maximise their revenues over their main trading period of August and early September. The business currently has three venues booked, with each venue able to hold up to 150 customers. The owners plan to run 45 screenings at each venue using their current resources and staff."},{"t":"p","x":"Starlight Classics operates with a small full-time administration team who organise the events. It employs staff on short-term contracts to run the events. The owners of the business are currently considering developing their product to include:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1. Additional premium services such as a ‘VIP package’ that includes red carpet arrival and a limousine ride to and from the event."},{"t":"p","x":"OR 2. Offering additional ‘bolt-on’ services such as the hire of chairs and cushions to customers who have bought cheaper tickets."},{"t":"p","x":"The marketing budget for the campaign is £45 000. The campaign should run for 26 weeks."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a pop-up cinema?"},{"t":"p","x":"This is when a film is screened in a temporary cinema set up in a space such as the courtyard of a building, the garden of a stately home, an old factory or other suitable venue. Events are often based on the theme of the film being shown."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":null}},{"id":"U2-2001","unit":2,"key":"2020-01","series":"January 2020","title":"Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms to promote its escape room business. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include: – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools – target market – size, structure and trends – competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms. The plan should include a:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Part A — independent research brief"},{"t":"p","x":"In this series Part A was a short brief: learners were asked to research the market for the business named in Part B independently (competitors, market size, share and structure, target market, trends, external influences and an example campaign), considering the reliability and validity of the data they collected, and to prepare up to six sides of A4 notes to take into the 3-hour Part B session."},{"t":"p","x":"The Part B scenario and the two activities (rationale and campaign plan) follow. Use the Part B information, and for practice research the market yourself, or try the more recent series that include a ready-made research pack."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms to promote its escape room business."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include: – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools – target market – size, structure and trends – competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include a:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The market for escape rooms in the UK In 2018 an entrepreneur bought a licence to use the characters and sets from the popular TV series ‘Sherlock’ as the basis for a series of escape room puzzles. Customers enter the room in a team of between four and six people. Their challenge lasts 90 minutes at a cost of £54 per person. The Sherlock challenge became the latest in a line of themed escape rooms."},{"t":"p","x":"The opening of the Sherlock escape room came at a point when the market for escape rooms had been through a significant period of growth. At a time when consumers are changing their shopping habits, moving towards online retail and looking for ‘experiences’ on the high street, these businesses have been in the right place at the right time."},{"t":"p","x":"Market research indicates that:"},{"t":"li","x":["there were seven escape rooms in the UK in 2013","there were over 1 200 escape rooms in the UK at the end of 2018","the market grew by 40% between 2017 and 2018","over 300 businesses operated in the escape room market in the UK in 2018","there were 450 distinct venues operating escape rooms with approximately 900 different themes offered at the end of 2018."]},{"t":"p","x":"Most escape rooms do not use well-known brands as part of their challenges. However, following the success of the Sherlock escape room, a Doctor Who themed challenge was launched."},{"t":"p","x":"The majority of the escape rooms in the UK are set up at a permanent location. Some escape rooms operate as a ‘pop-up’ business, running a challenge in a particular location for a short period of time before moving to a new city. Sometimes they take advantage of a unique venue that might only be available for a short period of time. Businesses operating escape rooms provide experiences for a range of target markets including:"},{"t":"li","x":["family activities","social events/parties","corporate team building events."]},{"t":"p","x":"Themes for games are highly varied with some based on murder mysteries, alien invasions, spy missions, zombie apocalypse and a range of other topics. In some cases, the theme is linked to the location of the game, using local landmarks or legends. Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms runs escape rooms in the UK. It operates a permanent site in a city centre location offering customers a choice of different escape room challenges. These range from an easy challenge that lasts an hour to an extreme challenge that lasts for two hours."},{"t":"p","x":"The business has created a brand image as a family-friendly company that provides fun for groups of between four and six people. Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms charges £30 per person for playing the game. In addition, it also sells a range of merchandise. This includes hooded sweatshirts with the Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms logo and team photographs as a reminder of the time that groups spent in the room."},{"t":"p","x":"6 The business is promoted in a variety of ways including:"},{"t":"li","x":["an e-commerce website that allows customers the chance to buy tickets","adverts in local newspapers","providing free tickets to YouTube influencers who then visit the escape room and vlog about their experience."]},{"t":"p","x":"Achieving business growth The owners of Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms want to grow their business. They currently have one location."},{"t":"p","x":"Cryptic Adventurers Escape Rooms has a small management team of permanent staff who create the puzzles and arrange the layout of the rooms. It also has a larger group of staff on zero-hour contracts to help look after customers."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of the business are considering two different options to support the expansion of the business."},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1. Gaining market share by offering special deals to university students. OR 2. Increasing revenue by selling escape room packages to businesses as corporate team building experiences."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of the business have allocated a budget of £25 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 52 weeks."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":null}},{"id":"U2-2101","unit":2,"key":"2021-01","series":"January 2021","title":"Team Ocuppola — esports team","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Team Ocuppola to develop brand awareness of its esports team. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• research data on the market, to include: – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools – target market – size, structure and trends – competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Team Ocuppola. The plan should include an appropriate:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Team Ocuppola.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Team Ocuppola that operates an esports team."},{"t":"p","x":"The business owners want to build brand awareness."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to independently research and analyse the market for esports in the UK. Your research should include primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences."]},{"t":"p","x":"You should research at least one marketing campaign related to the market for esports. You may wish to consider researching weekly/monthly marketing journals, marketing and/or advertising agency websites, business consultancies, and more general business sources."},{"t":"p","x":"Your research should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["costs","timescale","media used","message communicated","marketing mix","appropriateness of the campaign."]},{"t":"p","x":"You should take into consideration the validity and reliability of the data that you collect. You do not have to produce the promotional materials."},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of six sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Team Ocuppola to develop brand awareness of its esports team."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","research data on the market, to include: – an analysis of your research, using appropriate tools – target market – size, structure and trends – competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information researched","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Team Ocuppola."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include an appropriate:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Team Ocuppola."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"The market for esports in the UK In 2019 the esports industry was worth $1.1 billion globally. Over a quarter of this, around $264 million, was earned in Europe. This is a fast-growing market. It is well established in Asian countries and rapidly growing in popularity in the UK, Europe and the USA. In 2018, revenues from esports in the UK were £22 million. This was a 450% increase from 2014."},{"t":"p","x":"The esports industry generates revenue in a number of ways, including:"},{"t":"li","x":["events","merchandise sales","sponsorship","advertising"]},{"t":"p","x":"Esports events are a large and growing phenomenon. The biggest of these events are held in stadiums and arenas as well as being broadcast online. These venues are normally used for traditional major sporting events such as Premier League football or concerts by major music stars. In 2019, ‘The International’ was held in the Mercedes-Benz Arena in Shanghai. The arena holds around 18 000 people and tickets to the event sold out. Prizes for this type of event are large and esports teams compete to win millions of dollars. In 2019, the Fortnite World Cup awarded prizes worth a total of $100 million. One of the biggest esports events of the year is the Intel Extreme Masters series. This event is the equivalent of football’s FIFA World Cup. The finals are held annually in Katowice, Poland. In 2018 over 2.2 million people watched broadcasts of the events online. This had increased to 5 million in 2019. In 2020 Coronavirus (Covid-19) led to the stadium being closed to the public for the Intel Extreme Masters series final. Despite this, the event still set an all-time record for online viewers."},{"t":"p","x":"Broadcasts of esports events are increasingly popular. Globally, over 385 million people watched esports online in 2019 using platforms such as Twitch and YouTube. In the UK, over 4 million people watched esports in 2018. This is equivalent to 7% of the adult population. The majority of the esports viewers in the UK are aged between 18–24 (35%) or 25–34 (37%). Male viewers account for 69% of the audience with female viewers making up 31%. Fans of esports are often physically active and around 59% both watch and take part in traditional sports regularly. Research says that they do not watch much traditional television. Some estimates suggest that they watch just 3.8 hours per week. The UK has a large number of esports teams that compete in events globally. Some of the most well-known teams are:"},{"t":"li","x":["Team Endpoint","Fnatic","Prophecy","OpTic Gaming","Cloud 9","Team Dignitas"]},{"t":"p","x":"Team Dignitas is one of the most well-known and successful esports teams in the UK. It was first established in 2003 and has built a strong brand. Team Dignitas has gained sponsorship from a number of global brands, including Alienware (a maker of gaming PCs) and Mountain Dew (an American soft drink manufacturer)."},{"t":"p","x":"6 Team Ocuppola Team Ocuppola is an esports team based in the UK. It has been established for five years and the team has won a number of competitions in the UK and Europe. Team Ocuppola is beginning to build a social media following. Its members have over 1000 subscribers on Twitch."},{"t":"p","x":"The team is planning to enter more international competitions in 2021 and will be playing in events in Europe and Asia. The team currently has three sponsorship deals with British businesses. It also sells merchandise at events and through an online shop hosted on its website."},{"t":"p","x":"Gaining brand awareness Two of the biggest sources of revenue for an esports team are advertising and sponsorship. In order to attract lucrative sponsorship deals, Team Ocuppola will need to build strong brand awareness. This will also help the team to sell merchandise. The members of the team play a number of different games including League of Legends, Overwatch, Call of Duty and Fortnite. They are not planning to compete on any other games, but they do want to enter more competitions globally. The owners of the team believe that if the team wins tournaments it will help build a buzz on social media that will improve awareness of their brand."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of the team are considering two main options for building awareness of their brand:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1. Direct marketing activity targeting potential sponsors OR 2. Promoted posts on social media aimed at increasing the number of followers for the team."},{"t":"p","x":"The marketing budget for the campaign is £250 000. The campaign is expected to run for 12 months."},{"t":"p","x":"What is an esport?"},{"t":"p","x":"When multiplayer computer games are played competitively by professional players for an audience of spectators."}],"prep":{"pack":false,"sides":"six"}},{"id":"U2-2201","unit":2,"key":"2022-01","series":"January 2022","title":"Decreal Gear — sportswear","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for the owners of Decreal Gear to promote their sportswear. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign. You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Decreal Gear.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Decreal Gear. The business produces and sells sportswear."},{"t":"p","x":"The business owners want to increase revenue."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use given research independently to analyse the market for sportswear in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research will include primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","costs","timescale","media options."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"Sportswear Market in the UK By 2023, the market for sportswear in the UK is predicted to achieve a growth of 21%, according to GlobalData. An estimated £6.7 billion is forecast to be spent on sportswear in the UK. The growth in sportswear is likely to be higher than in all other areas of retail. JD Sports and Sports Direct are the two leading sportswear retailers in the UK, with shops located across the country. In 2020, JD Sports had a £6.1 billion worldwide revenue with Sports Direct achieving £3.9 billion. In addition, Statista report that online purchasing has become more popular, with 55% of people in the UK using the internet to make purchases of clothes, shoes and associated items."},{"t":"p","x":"The most popular participation sports in the UK in 2020, according to Statista were:"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Sport Number of","participants","Sport Number of","participants","Running 7 million General fitness 2.2 million","Fitness classes 6.4 million Football 1.4 million","Gym sessions 4 million Golf 0.8 million","Exercise machines 3.3 million Tennis 0.7 million","Hill walking 2.9 million Boxing 0.6 million","Interval sessions 2.6 million Badminton 0.6 million","Weight sessions 2.4 million Rowing 0.4 million","Swimming 2.4 million"]},{"t":"p","x":"The growth of sportswear was influenced by the COVID-19 pandemic. Many people were spending more time at home as a result of national/local lockdowns and working from home. This resulted in people wearing casual clothing and sportswear worn for leisure more often. This continued post-lockdown, with people being accustomed to wearing sportswear for multi purposes – to exercise, to wear casually during the day and even as part of an evening outfit."},{"t":"p","x":"The closure of sports facilities and gyms also changed the way that many people engaged in exercise, with home exercise and outdoor pursuits becoming more popular. The pandemic made people more aware of their health and wellbeing. Market analyst Kayla Marci stated that in 2020 there was an increase in purchases of sportswear for relaxing or exercising at home, for instance jogging bottoms, t-shirts, hooded sweatshirts and leggings. In the first quarter of 2020 the sales of men’s sportswear grew by 18% while women’s was up 43% in comparison to the same quarter of 2019. According to Pi Datametrics, the top 10 most popular online searches related to sportswear were loungewear, women’s tracksuits, men’s tracksuits, running shoes, sports bras, pyjamas for women, joggers, loungewear sets and gym leggings."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2020 people became more interested in choosing exercise linked to improving wellbeing to help manage their stress and anxiety. An impact of this was an overall 36% increase year on year in the sale of yoga sportswear, with a 45% increase in yoga-wear for men. 2021 saw an increase in people buying sportswear for the outdoors, with sports jackets and running clothes being very popular. Retailers are stocking 14% more loungewear products in 2021 compared to 2020."},{"t":"p","x":"The most popular sports clothing brands in the UK in 2021 were Nike, Adidas, Puma, Reebok, Slazenger, Lacoste and Fred Perry, with a large number of other branded competitors in the sportswear industry. These brands ranged from high street to luxury brands as well as sustainable sportswear businesses such as BAM, Contur, Olly Olly & Co, SOS Activewear and Tala, who use recycled and/or sustainable materials. Some of the 2021 trends in the sportswear industry were:"},{"t":"li","x":["Collaborating with fashion designers. Japanese designer Nigo has designed a range called “Human Wear” for Adidas. Victoria Beckham designs sportswear for Reebok.","Collaborating with celebrities and influencers. Alo Yoga associates with supermodels"]},{"t":"p","x":"Candice Swanepoel and the Hadid sisters. Supermodel Kate Moss and her daughter Lila are fans of PRISM Squared. PE Nation is worn by the Jenners and the Hadids."},{"t":"li","x":["Creating unisex sportswear items which can be worn by either male or female as designed by Spirit Jersey and Zoezi Sport.","A focus on black and white colours, as favoured by brands such as Kith and Rick"]},{"t":"p","x":"Owens, or a focus on pastel colours, used by brands such as FP Movement."},{"t":"li","x":["Using colour blocks in the design, as used by brands like Port De Bras and"]},{"t":"p","x":"Outdoor Voices."},{"t":"li","x":["The use of technology such as special fabrics that absorb sweat and odours or 3D knitting."]},{"t":"p","x":"According to Kayla Marci, the average price of women’s sportswear in the UK increased in"},{"t":"tbl","x":["Spring 2020, in comparison with Spring 2019.","Women’s Spring 2019 Spring 2020 Men’s Spring 2019 Spring 2020","Hooded tops £60 £68 Hooded tops £68 £67","Leggings £53 £61 Leggings £53 £64","Shorts £32 £35 Shorts £39 £39","Jogging","bottoms £55 £57 Jogging","bottoms £62 £62","T-shirts £28 £29 T-shirts £31 £30"]},{"t":"p","x":"Retailers offered bigger discounts in Spring 2020 than in the previous year, with average discounts of between 32–42% off the full price."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2020, some brands including Nike and Gymshark adapted their marketing messages towards supporting customers through the first Covid-19 lockdown. Nike created a social media campaign called “Play inside, play for the world” , encouraging people to stay at home. Nike also gave free access to Nike Training Club apps, with hundreds of home workouts and health advice. Gymshark temporarily changed its brand name on Twitter to Homeshark, to remind everyone to stay at home and continued to build relationships with customers by providing online access to home workouts, healthy recipes and tips to support mental health."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2021 sportswear marketing messages have evolved to promote both outdoor exercise and home exercise. Some brands have also used themes of diversity in their advertising. Many brands have introduced a more inclusive range of product sizing and used models of different shapes and sizes to reinforce the message that exercise is for everyone. Brands such as Nike have promoted their maternity range of exercise clothing, using images of heavily pregnant females participating in exercise. Further examples of diversity in sportswear advertising comes from Sweaty Betty’s “#RespectYourSweat” campaign, which shows images of a boxer wearing a hijab, sports people with disabilities and an older person on roller skates."},{"t":"p","x":"Decreal Gear: Market Research Report Date: Saturday 25th September 2021 Sample size: 100 Location: City centre"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"The sample: People aged 17–35 who exercised on average twice a week or more Summary of questionnaire responses 1 Sex:","c":["Male","Female","Other"],"v":["52","45","3"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"2 How often do you exercise on average per week?","c":["2 times per week","3–4 times per week","5–6 times per week","Every day"],"v":["21","36","34","9"]}},{"t":"tbl","x":["3 What type of exercise do you enjoy (answer as many as relevant)?","Gym/weights (in a gym or at home): 61","Cardio/Exercise classes (in a gym or at home): 30","Yoga/Pilates/Tai Chi 26","Running 36","Cycling 31","Football/Rugby 34","Netball/Hockey/Other team sports 21"]},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"4 How much do you spend per year on sportswear (excluding footwear)?","c":["0–£50","£51–£100","£101–£150","£151–£200","£201–£250","£251–£300","£301 +"],"v":["2","7","14","24","31","13","9"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"5 How important is branded sportswear?","c":["I rarely buy branded sportswear","I sometimes buy branded sportswear","I often buy sportswear from different brands","I usually buy sportswear from one brand"],"v":["5","16","45","34"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"6 Thinking about your favourite sportswear brand, what entices you to purchase that brand (answer all relevant)?","c":["Quality","Price","Fashionable","Celebrity endorsed","Environment factors","Technology enhanced"],"v":["81","24","71","56","36","67"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"7 What would enhance your relationship with a brand (answer all relevant)?","c":["Training videos","Fitness trackers","Access to sports experts","Wellness and stress support","Loyalty scheme","Discounts","Nutrition advice","Being part of an online community"],"v":["67","86","43","34","95","99","23","31"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Decreal Gear has researched the following options for increasing revenue through increased brand awareness and loyalty for their sportswear products. 1 Influencers"},{"t":"li","x":["Cost – varies depending on influencer – typically £110 per post.","Typically generates between 3 000–15 000 new followers per month depending on the influencer. 2 TikTok ads","Tik Tok has 100 million European users/community,","TikTok has 3.7 million active users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 41 minutes a day. This is lower than the global average TikTok user, who spends 52 minutes actively using the app.","In 2019, 26% of TikTok users were aged 18–24 and 9.3% were 25–34 in the UK, accessing TikTok from smartphones. 3 Google ads: Pay per click – with spend cap","Monthly budget cap which you can vary month by month.","Budget of £102 /month will generate estimated clicks or calls of 140–235","Google ads allows you to do your own demographic targeting, e.g. age, gender, household income, parental status. 4 Y ouTube","Allows you to set a budget – daily budget from £6 dependent on exposure needed.","More than 35.6 million adults in the UK use the platform.","35% of the UK population are active users of YouTube.","46% of women use YouTube in the UK vs 54% of men.","YouTube use is growing the fastest amongst Baby Boomers and Gen-X-ers.","The most active demographic is aged 25–44, accounting for 44% of all users.","The most popular YouTube channel in the UK was Ed Sheeran with 43 million subscribers. The channel also boasted around 18.78 billion views. 5 Trade/consumer fairs","There are many trade and consumer exhibitions that showcase health and fitness products, some of which are focused on particular areas of fitness, such as weight training, cardio classes, running or yoga.","International Fitness Showcase – Blackpool","Elevate – London"]},{"t":"li","x":["Bodypower Experience – Birmingham","Run Fit Expo – Liverpool","National Running Show – Birmingham and Farnborough","OM Yoga Show – London and Manchester","A stand at an exhibition in the UK typically costs £300–£350 per square metre, so even a small exhibition stand is likely to cost around £3 000.","This is a direct route to the UK gym and fitness market – connect directly with hundreds of thousands of consumers, influencers and trade buyers.","Create brand awareness and showcase your products alongside the latest trends in health and fitness.","Source fresh leads from an industry guest-list packed full of the leading professionals from across the UK. 6 Print media","Flyers – A5 premium glossy 1 000 – £30.51; 5 000 – £79.62","Business cards – 100 from £12.65 7 Billboards","Billboard rental costs in the UK vary according to the size and location of the billboard. Typically, a standard billboard costs £500–£600 for two weeks and smaller advertising panels like bus stop shelter advertisements cost £240–£270 for two weeks. 8 Other","Video production – £500 – £10 000","Animation – from £1 000","Voice over – from £500","Motion graphics – from £3 000","Filming – from £2 000 min","Stock images/video £500"]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and the notes and annotations from Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for the owners of Decreal Gear to promote their sportswear."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign."},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Decreal Gear."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"UK sportswear market The UK sportswear market is estimated to be worth £6.7 billion by 2023. This amount represents a growth of nearly 21% between 2018 and 2023. The growth is influenced by three main factors:"},{"t":"li","x":["an increase in people doing exercise to get fit","more people working from home and choosing to wear comfortable sportswear","the impact of celebrities and influencers being photographed wearing sportswear."]},{"t":"p","x":"The UK sportswear market is well established. Adidas and Nike dominate the UK market and other well-known brands, including Puma, Lululemon, Gymshark, and Sweaty Betty compete for market share. The market includes a number of independent brands. These brands often focus on a particular target market such as yoga, skate, gym or dance enthusiasts."},{"t":"p","x":"Many brands use social media to build customer relationships. Nike has an app that features over 190 different workouts, including gym, yoga and cardio training. Nike also publishes running tips on its website. It offers a progress tracker app with a facility to share achievements with friends. Gymshark features training workouts and nutritional advice from influencers and bloggers on its social media platforms. Gymshark also provides information about mental health and wellbeing to support customers. Around two thirds of sportswear consumers exercise several times a week. Brands typically launch new products linked to the staging of major sporting events. There is usually a short-term increase in the level of exercise during and after those events. Sportswear brands sometimes partner with fashion designers or celebrities to extend their product lines. Nike collaborated with four leading female designers to create a range of football inspired sportswear for the 2019 FIFA Women’s World Cup. Adidas has a lifetime sponsorship deal with footballer Lionel Messi. It offers a range of competitively priced sportswear featuring the Messi brand."},{"t":"p","x":"Most major sportswear brands sell through retail outlets. They also sell online via their own websites and through other websites such as Amazon. The amount of online sales as a percentage of total sales has continued to rise in recent times. Smaller independent brands often sell directly to customers online. Some also sell through online clothing retailers."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Figure 1 shows the average price of sportswear.","Product Average price","Spring 2020","Average price","Spring 2019","Hoodies £68 £60","Sports leggings £61 £53","Joggers £57 £55","Shorts £35 £32","T-shirts £29 £28","Figure 1"]},{"t":"p","x":"Sportswear brands offered discounts of an average of 38% off products in April 2020. Many sportswear brands offer discounts to students, apprentices, fitness industry workers and other key customers. Some brands offer discounts on the first purchase. Other brands provide a customer loyalty scheme collecting points that can be used to obtain a discount on future purchases."},{"t":"p","x":"Decreal Gear Decreal Gear is an independent sportswear company that designs and makes its own products."},{"t":"p","x":"Figure 2 shows the price range of different products for Decreal Gear in Spring 2020."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Product Price","Spring 2020","Hoodies £40–£50","Sports leggings £25–£45","Joggers £30–£45","Shorts £20–£35","T-shirts £15–£30","Figure 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Decreal Gear has an excellent reputation for quality and its clothing range is stylish. The company collaborates with a number of graduate designers. Decreal Gear sells exclusively through its own website. It aims to deliver within 48 hours. Delivery is free and customers can also return items free of charge if they are not happy with them."},{"t":"p","x":"Research undertaken by Decreal Gear suggests that its customers are mainly fitness enthusiasts, aged 25–35. A few customers are fitness influencers in the UK, with their own YouTube and Instagram accounts. Despite this popularity amongst fitness enthusiasts and influencers, sales revenues at Decreal Gear have not grown over the past three years."},{"t":"p","x":"Increasing revenue The owners of Decreal Gear want to increase revenue through increased brand awareness and loyalty. They want to build relationships with their existing customers. They believe that the trend for staying healthy and wearing sportswear for exercise and everyday use will continue. They want to increase sales from existing customers by encouraging them to interact with the company’s social media platforms. They also want to attract more new customers."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Decreal Gear are considering two options to help them achieve their goals. EITHER 1. To create content for social media to provide information about fitness, training programmes and other useful advice."},{"t":"p","x":"OR 2. To introduce a loyalty scheme for existing and new customers. The marketing budget for the campaign is £20 000. The campaign should run for thirty weeks."},{"t":"p","x":"What is sportswear?"},{"t":"p","x":"Clothing worn for sport or physical activities or casual indoor or outdoor use."}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-2206","unit":2,"key":"2022-06","series":"June 2022","title":"Wholly Nutritious — food boxes","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Wholly Nutritious to promote its food box business. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign. You must produce this plan in an appropriate format for the owners of Wholly Nutritious.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Wholly Nutritious that delivers boxes of food."},{"t":"p","x":"The business owners want to maintain their revenues."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use given research independently to analyse the market for food boxes in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research will include primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","costs","timescale","media","message communicated","marketing mix","appropriateness of the campaign."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"Research pack Market for food boxes in the UK The food box market is estimated to exceed £1.5 billion within the UK in 2022, with sales of food boxes expected to rise 55% year on year. The sudden increase in the demand for food and recipe boxes has led to rapid growth within the market and has led to a significant rise in the number of local, national and international food box brands. Approximately 26% of households are now purchasing food boxes weekly. The market is dominated by leading brands HelloFresh, Gousto and Mindful Chef, with an increase in the number of niche food box providers supplying specialised boxes such as Japanese, organic and vegan meals."},{"t":"p","x":"Market leader HelloFresh has recently reported an increase in demand and now provides 250,000 food boxes each month. Research by Statistica has shown that over the past year alone food box subscriptions have increased by 48% and have become the leading type of subscription box around the globe. The Guild of Fine Food supports such findings by highlighting that 36% of consumers now use the internet to source ingredients, recipes or meal kits in the form of food boxes."},{"t":"p","x":"Mindful Chef is another food box company. Its most recent market share report has highlighted that its food boxes are most popular with those in the 18–34 age group, with 18% of those signing up to more than one food box service. In contrast, only 3% of those over the age of 55 use the service."},{"t":"p","x":"The concept of food boxes is not something new and some of the larger brands have been in operation since 2015. However, the past three years have led to changes in social trends, such as more people cooking at home and an increase in demand for the direct delivery of recipes and ingredients. Society has become more focused on enjoying cooking, which has resulted in a decrease of takeaway sales and an increase in the popularity of food boxes."},{"t":"p","x":"Research by the Financial Times shows that most people who purchase food boxes do so for the following reasons:"},{"t":"li","x":["The ease of having food and recipes delivered to their door.","The enjoyment of being able to cook different recipes each week.","The novelty and surprise of having meals delivered.","The ability to try new foods and experiment with different ingredients.","The access to high-quality products and restaurant-standard meals."]},{"t":"p","x":"Some younger consumers also highlight that staying on trend is a key reason for subscribing to food boxes and will subscribe to multiple food boxes if there are offers and promotions."},{"t":"p","x":"More recently, supermarkets have challenged the food box market with both Marks & Spencer (M&S) and Morrisons launching meal kit boxes that consumers can cook and enjoy at home. M&S offers a selection of meals for two that include a starter, main, side and dessert, relying on its brand image, loyal customer base and guarantee of 100% British ingredients to attract buyers. Whereas Morrisons has specifically targeted the family market with food boxes that provide a family of four with five meals."},{"t":"p","x":"HelloFresh The market leader in the UK is HelloFresh that offers weekly food boxes for two to four people. Boxes are priced at between £42.99 and £60.99. HelloFresh has a menu of over 37 recipes each week and offers themed and seasonal dishes throughout the year. HelloFresh 2021/22 product range Product Description Veggie Box A selection of up to five vegetarian and meat-free alternative meals each week."},{"t":"p","x":"Family Box A selection of up to five meals that contains meals from the full menu, including sweet dishes."},{"t":"p","x":"Quick Cook Box A selection of up to five easy to prepare and quick to cook meals including cold dishes."},{"t":"p","x":"Calorie Smart Box A selection of five meals that are under 600 calories per serving and have health benefits."},{"t":"p","x":"HelloFresh has a large social media following and regularly uses influencers to showcase its recipes. More recently, the brand has changed its marketing message to highlight the social benefits of its products. The ‘Fresh from the ‘gram’ message has directly encouraged consumers to connect and interact through Instagram to share their stories and showcase the power of the products in bringing people together. As part of its most successful marketing campaign, HelloFresh linked with celebrities who share their experiences of preparing, cooking, and eating different recipes over a 21-day period using the hashtag #RefreshWithHelloFresh on a range of social media platforms. The campaign was targeted at young adults and health-conscious families. Gousto Gousto was the first UK-based food box service. It offers a menu of up to 60 recipes, which consumers can select for themselves. Boxes are priced between £24.99 and £47.75. Gousto offers festive meals, calorie controlled and healthy meals alongside branded Lean in 15 recipes endorsed by Joe Wicks."},{"t":"p","x":"Gousto 2021/22 product range Product Description Two-Person Box Offering two, three or four recipes for two people each week, which can be selected by the consumer from the main menu."},{"t":"p","x":"Family Box Offering two, three or four recipes for two adults and up to three children each week. Recipes can be selected by the consumer."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2021 Gousto launched its ‘Give it Some’ campaign that was used to challenge the opinion that food boxes can become repetitive and boring. The campaign used a combination of music and celebrities to liken food taste and the products to music tastes with the message of ‘whatever your taste, we’ve got something for you’ . Interactions on"},{"t":"p","x":"social media reached over one million and product sales reached a point where Gousto had to stop food box subscriptions for new customers."},{"t":"p","x":"The brand now has a heavily digital marketing strategy that makes use of social media platforms and post-sharing to reach new potential customers. However, its main aim is to retain customers and reduce subscription cancellations through customer engagement and innovation."},{"t":"p","x":"Smaller providers often have higher priced products due to their bespoke nature, with some charging £16.99 for a meal for two and others charging a different price for each meal selected. Most food box businesses offer discounts for new customers and offer incentives for those who encourage others to subscribe."},{"t":"p","x":"However, the long-term outlook for food boxes is not as positive say Delish.com, a specialist magazine on trends in food and drink. On average less than 20% of UK customers have kept their subscription services for more than a year, with research showing that the average consumer cancels their subscriptions within five months. Most consumers highlight that their reason for cancelling is that the food boxes become repetitive and the meal choice is not sufficient. 46% of consumers state their cancellation is due to financial reasons. It is no surprise therefore that the market leaders in food boxes state customer retention as their key focus for the coming year. In a recent report, a global food and drink analyst suggested that the success of any food box company is based on customer retention. They stated that food boxes are notably expensive to produce and rely on the consumer ‘wanting’ to stay in and cook, which limits their ability to be spontaneous. Therefore, food box brands now need to think outside the box and consider what other products, add-ons and benefits they can link to their products in order to sustain and maintain custom and relationships. Food box brands have also come under more intense scrutiny due to their excessive use of single-use plastics. Consumers are calling for market leaders to review how they package ingredients and print recipe cards to reduce the amount of waste. As part of the growing concerns for the environment, the UK Government has started a consultation on single-use plastic packaging. The consultation will challenge food box businesses to consider their carbon footprint and their impact on the environment. Gousto has responded by cutting back on all packaging and is using environmentally friendly materials across all food boxes, which has cut plastic use by 50%. Riverford has also responded by launching a recycling guide to show consumers what can be reused, recycled and returned. The company has also pledged to remove all plastic packaging from its fruit and vegetables."},{"t":"p","x":"Wholly Nutritious market research report Day: Saturday Sample size: 80 Location: Food market based in a busy city centre"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Sample: Customers between the ages of 20 and 50 Summary of questionnaire responses Gender:","c":["Male","Female","Other"],"v":["46","33","1"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age:","c":["20–29","30–39","40–49","50+"],"v":["10","35","28","7"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How often do you order food boxes?","c":["Once per month or less","Twice per month","Once per week"],"v":["9","22","49"]}},{"t":"p","x":"If you were purchasing a food box that provides two main meals for one person how much would you be willing to pay? £10 – £15 £16 – £25 £26+ 22 37 21 What type of food boxes do you prefer? (Select all that apply.) Mixed boxes (whole range) Vegetarian boxes Family boxes Low calorie boxes 56 25 42 49 Vegan boxes Dairy free boxes Quick cook boxes Dessert boxes 9 2 46 37"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which of the following is the most important factor when considering a food box?","c":["The range of meal choices","The ease of purchase and delivery","The freshness of ingredients","The cooking experience","The health benefits","The price of the food box"],"v":["27","11","14","4","6","18"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How interested would you be in online cooking demonstrations linked to your food boxes?","c":["Not interested","Somewhat interested","Very interested"],"v":["24","39","17"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How important are celebrity recommendations when selecting a food box?","c":["Not important","Somewhat important","Very important"],"v":["23","32","25"]}},{"t":"p","x":"What would persuade you to maintain an ongoing food box subscription? (Select all that apply.) New recipes Seasonal dishes Themed recipes Unique ingredients 49 62 22 27 Special offers Discounted pricing Free samples Loyalty scheme 54 39 24 8"},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Wholly Nutritious has researched the following options for improving customer engagement: 1 TikTok ads"},{"t":"li","x":["TikTok has 100 million European users/community.","TikTok has 3.7 million active users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 41 minutes a day. This is lower than the global average TikTok user, who spends 52 minutes actively using the app.","In 2019 and 2020, 9.3% of users in the UK were aged 25–34. 2 Facebook","Average Facebook ads cost £0.78 per click.","More than 48 million people in the UK use Facebook. Approximately 44% of people use Facebook every day.","26% of users are aged 25–34 and 19% are aged 35–44.","In households with one or more children, users access Facebook for 2–3 hours each per day. 3 Google ads: Pay per click – with spend cap","Monthly budget cap that you can vary month by month.","Budget of £200/month will generate an estimated 180–270 clicks or calls.","Google ads allow you to do your own demographic targeting, e.g. – Age: 18–24, 25–34, 35–44, 45–54, 55–64, 65+ and Unknown – Gender: Female, Male and Other – Household income: Top 10% of earners, 11–20%, 21–30%, 31–40%, 41–50%,"]},{"t":"p","x":"Lower 50% and Unknown – Parental status: Parent, Not a parent and Unknown 4 Y ouTube Allows you to set a budget – daily budget from £6 depending on exposure needed"},{"t":"li","x":["More than 35.6 million adults in the UK use the platform.","35% of the UK population are an active user of YouTube.","46% of women use YouTube in the UK vs 54% of men.","YouTube use is growing the fastest amongst Baby Boomers and Gen-X-ers.","The most active demographic is aged 25–44, accounting for 44% of all users.","The most popular YouTube channel in the UK was Ed Sheeran with 43 million subscribers. The channel also boasted around 18.78 billion views."]},{"t":"p","x":"5 Snapchat ads"},{"t":"li","x":["Monthly budget cap that you can vary month by month.","Average Snapchat ads cost £0.56 per click/swipe.","More than 20.1 million people in the UK are active users of Snapchat.","59% of users are aged 20–55.","Chance to connect with potential customers in real-time through the creation of filters, add-ons and interactive stories.","Opportunities to blend viral advertising with ecommerce and website directions.","Location based targeting through snap-map and locators. 6 Instagram ads","Average Instagram ad costs £0.35–£0.75 per click.","More than 30 million users in the UK with 54% using Instagram several times a day.","30% of users are aged 25–34 and 57% of users are women. 7 Food festivals","There are approximately 180 food festivals in the UK where you can have a stall, sell products and meet your potential customers.","Food festivals are usually held in the summer and Christmas periods.","Local food festivals attract between 200 and 1 000 visitors, with national events attracting over 15 000 visitors per day.","Stalls cost from £250 to £1 500 and you need public liability insurance, a basic food hygiene certificate, stallholder insurance and you must follow guidance about selling food and drink.","Create brand awareness and showcase products alongside the latest trends in food and drink."]},{"t":"p","x":"8 BBC Good Food Show"},{"t":"li","x":["Large food-based event held in major UK cities in July and November.","Attracts around 150 000 visitors over four days with live demonstrations, celebrity chefs and trade stands.","A stand typically costs £450–£600 per square metre, a small exhibition stand is likely to cost around £5 000.","70% of visitors are aged 30–65, with increasing numbers of families attending the events each year.","This is a way of engaging with potential customers over four days while showcasing your product within live demonstrations, celebrity chef promotions and free samples.","Creates brand awareness and showcases products alongside latest trends in food, drink and cooking. 9 Farmers’ markets","There are approximately 650 farmers’ markets in the UK where you can have a stall, sell products and meet potential customers.","Stalls cost from £10 to £75 and you need public liability insurance as well as a basic food hygiene certificate if you are selling or providing samples of cooked meals.","You can engage with potential customers and carry out demonstrations of meals that could be created from the food boxes. 10 Other","Video production – £500 to £10 000","Animation – from £1 000","Voice-over – from £500","Motion graphics – from £3 000","Filming – from £2 000 per minute","Stock images/video £500"]},{"t":"p","x":"What is a food box?"},{"t":"p","x":"A collection of fresh food, normally enough to last a week. Most contain some combination of fruit and vegetables. Sometimes other items such as meat, dairy products or dry goods are added."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a recipe box?"},{"t":"p","x":"A pack of ingredients with instructions for making a specific meal. What are seasonal goods?"},{"t":"p","x":"Products that are only available at certain times of the year or that are available in greater quantities at certain times of the year."}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and the notes and annotations from Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Wholly Nutritious to promote its food box business."},{"t":"p","x":"This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign."},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate format for the owners of Wholly Nutritious."},{"t":"h","x":"Part B Set Task Information"},{"t":"p","x":"UK food box market In 2020, demand for food boxes increased dramatically. Sales increased by 114% in April 2020 alone. The amount spent on these products by consumers increased by 36% during this time. Examples of these products include vegetable, recipe and meat boxes. One company, Gousto, earned more in the first six months of 2020 than in the whole of 2019. It is reported that the owners of that business plan to increase their production capacity by 300%."},{"t":"p","x":"Sales of food boxes increased during 2019 and 2020 as part of a growing trend for people to do their grocery shopping online. Large numbers of people who were unable to visit shops started to buy groceries online. However, in the second half of 2020 some people started going to physical shops again, but many continued doing their grocery shopping online. Industry experts believe that the positive experience of shopping online during this time accelerated a long-term trend for people to do more shopping online. The market is very competitive. Most companies tend to focus on selling food boxes to a specific part of the country before expanding to other areas. Many brands in this market try to differentiate themselves from their competitors."},{"t":"li","x":["Oddbox focuses on selling to customers in London and Brighton. It sells boxes of fruit and vegetables that have been rejected by supermarkets due to being unusual shapes.","Riverford covers the whole of the UK. It offers both vegetable and recipe boxes that contain organic products. Its boxes mainly contain seasonal products.","Mindful Chef offers recipe boxes throughout the UK. Its boxes focus on food that is nutritious and low in carbohydrates. They are also free from gluten and dairy."]},{"t":"p","x":"The success of smaller food box businesses has led major retail chains to consider launching products in this market. In March 2020, Morrisons launched its ‘Essentials Box’ which gave customers a choice of options either containing meat or that were meat- free. These boxes were priced at £35 and aimed to provide enough food to make three meals for a family of four. Morrisons boxes were so successful that its website crashed on launch day because of the extra traffic. The successful launch of these boxes led to the development of a range of ‘Square Meal’ recipe boxes. These are aimed at making classic British dishes such as shepherd’s pie or sausage and mash."},{"t":"p","x":"Food boxes tend to be sold online. People can either subscribe to regular deliveries or make one-off purchases of a single box. The popularity of these services has grown in line with the wider food retail market. Despite this, some smaller independent businesses also offer food boxes through retail shops."},{"t":"p","x":"A potential challenge to food retailing and the food box market is Amazon Go. The business has recently opened a checkout-less food store in London and plan to expand their offering across the UK. Customers download the Amazon Go app, do their shopping, and leave the store without going through a till. The app logs and charges for the products the customer takes out of the store."},{"t":"p","x":"Wholly Nutritious Wholly Nutritious runs a food box business in the UK. The business started on a farm in 2000, selling boxes of fruit and vegetables and delivering to customers within 50 miles. Between 2000 and 2015 the business grew, buying new premises and expanding to cover the whole of the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"Customers are offered a range of subscription options. They can choose either: – food boxes containing fruit and/or vegetables. These are priced at between £10 and £50 depending on size and content – recipe boxes containing ingredients and a recipe for a specific meal. These are priced between £5 and £30, depending on how many people the meal is intended to serve."},{"t":"p","x":"The business mainly advertises using promoted posts on social media. Current customers are sent emails about special offers. They are also given a code that they can pass on to friends. If their friend uses the code when they order a subscription they are both given a discount."},{"t":"p","x":"Customers purchase their boxes online. They can choose from 10 different types of food box and 50 different recipe boxes. Managers at Wholly Nutritious divide their target market into two groups: 1. Young professionals aged 21 to 45 who lead busy lives and want recipe kits to help them save time 2. Households with at least one child. These families want good-quality, fresh fruit and/or vegetables."},{"t":"p","x":"Maintaining revenue After a period of significant growth, the owners of Wholly Nutritious believe that the business should not grow any further in the short term as this may compromise the quality of the service that they provide."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of the business are currently considering developing their promotional activity to include:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER 1. Posting videos each day on social media showing how to cook meals using items from Wholly Nutritious boxes."},{"t":"p","x":"OR 2. Running stalls at local markets and food festivals to persuade people to subscribe to a food and/or recipe box delivery."},{"t":"p","x":"The marketing budget for the campaign is £150,000. The campaign should run for 52 weeks."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a food box?"},{"t":"p","x":"What is a recipe box?"}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-2301","unit":2,"key":"2023-01","series":"January 2023","title":"Flares Skatepark","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Flares Skatepark to increase the customer base. Your rationale should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign. You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the management team at Flares Skatepark.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Flares Skatepark. The business operates a skatepark."},{"t":"p","x":"The management team at Flares Skatepark wants to increase the customer base. You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for indoor skateparks in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research will include primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","costs","timescale","media","message communicated","marketing mix","appropriateness of the campaign."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"Skateparks in the UK There are an estimated 1 600 outdoor skateparks in the UK, run by local councils or community groups. They are usually unsupervised and they give free access to all users. There are 74 indoor skateparks in the UK and customers pay to use the facilities. Most indoor skateparks are run by charities and provide a safe, supervised place for young people to enjoy sport. Any profit the charities make is invested in maintaining and improving facilities and paying for staff to run the business. Skateparks are used by people who ride skateboards, BMX and other bicycles and adapted wheelchairs called WCMX. They are also used by skaters and people who ride kick scooters. Skateparks have positive benefits for the physical and mental health of the people who use them. They are affordable and accessible to people of all ages, genders and cultures."},{"t":"p","x":"Image 1 (Source: © FourOneFour Skateparks) Image 1 shows curved areas and street obstacles at an indoor skatepark. There are up to 750 000 skateboarders in the UK. An estimated 85% of these are male and 15% are female. It is suggested that there are around 54 000 people who are regular skaters, with the average age of active skateboarders being 14 years old. The popularity of skateboarding has fluctuated for decades. It is currently very popular due to being affordable and easy to do anywhere. The global skateboard market is estimated to reach $2.4 billion by 2025, which would be a year on year increase of 2%. BMX originated in California in the 1970s and became popular in the UK in 1980. In 2020 in the USA there were nearly 4 million BMX riders aged six years and over. In the UK, BMX clubs are reporting increased participation from new and experienced riders. Skateparks are now running BMX freestyle sessions for people aged over 40."},{"t":"p","x":"The Olympic Games increased the interest and popularity of both BMX riding and skateboarding. In 2008, the Beijing Olympic Games hosted the first BMX racing events for men and women. The 2020 Tokyo Olympic Games (postponed to summer 2021), introduced the first BMX Freestyle competition and the first skateboarding events for both men and women."},{"t":"p","x":"The UK won five Olympic medals at Tokyo:"},{"t":"li","x":["Bethany Shriever – Gold in Women’s BMX Racing","Kye Whyte – Silver in Men’s BMX Racing","Charlotte Worthington – Gold in Women’s Park BMX Freestyle","Declan Brooks – Bronze in Men’s Park BMX Freestyle","Sky Brown – Bronze in Women’s Street Skateboard."]},{"t":"p","x":"After the success of the UK BMX team at the Tokyo Olympic Games, British Cycling developed a UK freestyle competition structure to start in 2022. Its aim was to establish BMX freestyle centres and to develop talented BMX participants who could become the Olympic stars of the future."},{"t":"p","x":"The UK Government wants to improve the poor levels of participation in exercise by children and young people. In 2019, the UK Chief Medical Officers issued guidelines that children and young people should engage in moderate to vigorous activity for at least 60 minutes per day. This activity includes PE at school, after-school activities, play and sports. In the summer of 2020, approximately 50% of children and young people met this target."},{"t":"p","x":"Indoor skateparks in the UK Some of the popular indoor skateparks are Adrenaline Alley in Corby, RAMP 1 Skatepark in Warrington, Onboard Skatepark in Sheffield, Source BMX Skatepark in Hastings and Creation Skatepark in Birmingham."},{"t":"p","x":"Adrenaline Alley is a six-acre site and is Europe’s largest action sports venue. It has three buildings, allowing indoor riding for different abilities and includes foam pits to practise tricks safely. It has an outdoor pump track and street plaza, and an American diner selling food and drinks. Adrenaline Alley sells its own range of merchandise including T-shirts, hoodies, hats, beanies, accessories and gift vouchers. They hire out equipment such as BMX bikes, scooters, skateboards, inline blades, helmets and safety pads. They also rent a retail space to SkateHut, a business that sells a range of skateboards, scooters, inline skates, protection and clothing."},{"t":"p","x":"RAMP 1 hosts nine areas for different abilities and includes a 12 m skateable tunnel, one of the largest bowls ever constructed, ramps and a street plaza. It has a cafe, equipment hire, repair centre and a merchandise store."},{"t":"p","x":"Source BMX Skatepark is the world’s largest underground skatepark with ramps and bowls suitable for all abilities and ages. It offers specific sessions for skateboarders, BMX riders and mixed sessions open to everyone including inline skaters and scooter riders. Some indoor skateparks like Adrenaline Alley offer skateboarding, scooter and BMX lessons. Lessons focus on either beginners or on developing advanced skills and tricks. They have experienced coaches and charge approximately £45.50 for a seven-week course. RAMP 1 offers personal tuition at £25 per hour."},{"t":"p","x":"Some indoor skateparks host special events."},{"t":"li","x":["Creation Skatepark had an all-night event. Riders paid £14 to ride all night and they were given a voucher for a hot meal.","Adrenaline Alley hosted a UK WCMX Jam. This was a day of free activities for people with physical and learning disabilities.","Onboard Skatepark hosts meet and greet events giving customers the chance to meet and ride with professional riders for £20.","Adrenaline Alley ran a Christmas Camp with Olympic Bronze medallist Declan Brooks, priced at £65.","Some indoor skateparks run holiday ‘camps’ during the school holidays."]},{"t":"p","x":"The majority of indoor skateparks require customers to pre-book tickets online. Riders complete a registration form which confirms the centre cannot be held responsible for injuries and they have to agree to terms and conditions. Parental consent has to be given for under 18s."},{"t":"p","x":"Most indoor skateparks are staffed by paid employees and volunteers many of whom are riders themselves. They advise customers how to stay safe and how to use their facilities."},{"t":"tbl","x":["In June 2022, the following prices were charged by indoor skateparks.","Venue Session Price","Creation Skatepark, Birmingham","Compulsory Membership: £3.00 per year","Hire of scooter, skateboard or BMX: £6.00.","Helmet hire: £3.00","All day £9.50 – £11.50","Late session £6.00","Full week £25.00 – £30.00","Adrenaline Alley, Corby","Optional Membership: £25.00 per year for","10–15% off prices","Half day £20.00","All day £30.00","Late session £11.50","Saturday under 16s price £11.50","Onboard Skatepark, Sheffield","No membership required","3-hour session £10.00","All day £13.50","Full week £25.00"]},{"t":"p","x":"Most indoor skateparks have a limited marketing budget. At Adrenaline Alley, advertising is carefully targeted at young people aged 12–25 years old, using social media platforms such as Instagram and TikTok. They advertise on Google and Facebook, targeting the parents/guardians of children who live in the area. Customers are encouraged to post videos and pictures to promote the skatepark. The skatepark employs social media staff to repost videos and respond quickly to comments posted on social media about the venue. They spend money on professional videos, which are posted on their website and YouTube account."},{"t":"p","x":"The Adrenaline Alley team prints leaflets that are given to people at local family events. The leaflets often include a sales promotion offering money off with a voucher code. Posters are printed and displayed inside the venue to advertise forthcoming skatepark events. They regularly email their database of over 164 000 people. Adrenaline Alley contacts professional skateboarders, scooter and BMX riders and offers them private, free of charge sessions in the skatepark with their friends. They do not pay these influencers but benefit from the influencers posting videos on their personal social media sites. This is particularly effective if they are opening new areas of the skatepark that they want to showcase."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Flares Skatepark: Market Research Report","Date: Saturday 24th September 2022","Sample size: 100","Location: City Centre","The sample: Parents/guardians of children aged 10–17 years old"]},{"t":"p","x":"Summary of questionnaire responses:"},{"t":"tbl","x":["How often does your child exercise for at least 60 minutes per day?","0 times per week 1–2 times per","week","3–4 times per","week","5–6 times per","week","Every day","8 36 29 19 8"]},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How would you describe the amount of exercise your child does each day?","c":["My child should exercise more","My child does the right amount of exercise","My child does too much exercise"],"v":["58","39","3"]}},{"t":"p","x":"What is stopping your child from doing more exercise? (Answer as many as relevant.) Question asked only to a parent/guardian who stated their child should exercise more."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Too much homework 5","Prefers passive activities at home, e.g. gaming 47","Dislikes exercise 31","No suitable activities available in the area 56","Cannot afford for child to join sports clubs 24","Medical reasons 2"]},{"t":"p","x":"Has your child ridden a skateboard, scooter or bike in the last year? (Answer as many as relevant.) Skateboard Scooter Bike None 32 19 51 37 Rate from 1 to 5 how much you think your child would enjoy riding at a skatepark like this one. (Participants are shown pictures of Flares Skatepark.) 1 Would love it 2 3 4 5 Would hate it 15 44 20 16 5"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you expect to pay for a 3 hour session at a skatepark like this?","c":["£4.00–7.99","£8.00–11.99","£12.00–15.99","£16.00–19.99","£20.00+"],"v":["14","23","31","21","11"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What do you expect from a skatepark? (Answer all relevant.)","c":["Excellent health & safety standards","Staff who enjoy skating/riding","Access for all abilities","Cafe with good food and drink"],"v":["96","54","43","34"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Loyalty scheme Discounts and offers Area with Wi-Fi for parents to wait Clean and well maintained 35 99 67 31 Lessons Special events, e.g. meet and greet the stars Equipment hire Sessions for all ability levels, e.g. beginner 29 37 88 92"},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Flares Skatepark has researched the following options for increasing the customer base. 1. Facebook"},{"t":"li","x":["Average Facebook ads cost £0.78 per click.","More than 48 million people in the UK use the platform and roughly 44% of those use Facebook every day.","25% are aged 25–34 and 19% are aged 35–44. People aged 13–17 are the least likely to use Facebook. 2. YouTube","Allows you to set a budget – daily budget from £6 dependent on exposure needed.","More than 35.6 million adults in the UK use the platform.","35% of the UK population are an active user of YouTube.","46% of women in the UK and 54% of men use YouTube.","YouTube use is growing the fastest amongst Baby Boomers and Gen-X-ers.","The most active demographic are aged 25-44, accounting for 44% of all users.","Some of the most popular YouTube channels in the UK for BMX are: – Ryan Taylor with 2.36 million subscribers – Scotty Cranmer with 1.72 million subscribers – Ryan Williams with 1.64 million subscribers – Red Bull Bike with 1.64 million subscribers – Billy Perry with 1.38 million subscribers – Ride UK BMX, with 18 200 subscribers.","Some of the most popular YouTube channels in the UK for skateboarders are: – Braille Skateboarding with 5.73 million subscribers – Andrew Schrock with 4.74 million subscribers – Thrasher Magazine with 2.94 million subscribers – Ride Channel with 1.73 million subscribers – The Berrics with 1.36 million subscribers – Sam Tabor with 1.33 million subscribers. 3. Television advertising","There are two costs – production of the advert and buying advertisement slots.","Production varies from £25 000 for a basic 30-second advertisement to £250 000 for top stars and a recognised music track.","ITV daytime slots are £3 500–£4 500 and peak time (evening) slots are £10 000–£33 000 for a 30-second advert.","Costs are much less for the ITV channels like CITV, also letting you target a more specific market.","Channel 4 daytime slots cost £1 000-£2 000 with peak rates of £10 000–£20 000.","Channel 5 daytime slots are £800–£1 600 with peak rates of £2 500–£4 000.","Sky TV daytime slots are £150–£250 with peak rates of £650–£1 150.","Other digital channels cost £50–£150 for daytime slots and £150–£300 for peak time."]},{"t":"p","x":"4. Instagram ads"},{"t":"li","x":["Average Instagram ads costs £0.35–£0.75 per click.","More than 34 million users in the UK with 54% using the platform several times a day.","30% are aged 25–34 and 55% are women. 5. TikTok ads","Minimum £50 per day, with options to target by gender, age or interest.","TikTok has 17 million active users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 66 minutes a day.","In 2019, 26% of TikTok users in the UK were aged 18–24 and 9.3% were 25–34, accessing TikTok from smartphones.","65% of TikTok’s UK users are female and 35% are male. 6. Google Ads","Pay per click, with spend cap.","Monthly budget cap which you can vary month by month.","Budget of £102 per month will generate estimated clicks or calls of 140–235.","Google Ads allows you to do your own demographic targeting, e.g. age, gender, household income, parental status. 7. Influencers","Cost – varies depending on influencer – typically £80 per post per 10 000 followers.","Mid-tier influencers with 50 000–100 000 followers charge £600–£1 000 and mega influencers with 500 000 – 1 million followers charge £1 750–3 000 per post. 8. Billboards","Billboard rental costs in the UK vary according to the size and location of the billboard.","Typically, a standard billboard costs £500–£600 for two weeks and smaller advertising panels like bus stop shelter advertisements cost £240–£270 for two weeks. 9. Print media","Flyers – A5 premium glossy 1 000 – £32.03, 5 000 – £83.59","Vouchers – A6 standard 1 000 – £20.85, 5 000 – £46.04","Business cards – 100 from £10.05","Local newspapers – quarter page colour advertisement from £250 10. Spotify","Spotify has 422 million listeners worldwide who using Spotify to stream music and podcasts. Of these, 182 million premium subscribers listen advert-free.","Research has shown that Spotify is the most trusted social media platform for adverts.","Audio advert campaigns start at £250.","You can create your own voice-over advert using Spotify’s professional quality production tools at no extra cost.","You can target your audience by location, age, genre of music and device."]},{"t":"p","x":"11. Sponsorship"},{"t":"li","x":["Opportunities exist to sponsor sports teams, events and charities. Shirt sponsorship for a local football team will cost around £1 000. Sponsorship of a small local music festival will cost from £500-£5 000 depending upon the package. 12. Other","Staff to hand out leaflets/vouchers – £10 per person per hour","Video production – £500–£10 000","Animation – from £1 000","Voice-over – from £500","Motion graphics – from £3 000","Filming – from £2 000 minimum","Stock images/video £500"]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the extra task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Flares Skatepark to increase the customer base. Your rationale should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, develop a budgeted plan with a timescale for your marketing campaign."},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the management team at Flares Skatepark."},{"t":"p","x":"5 Skateparks in the UK There are 74 indoor skateparks in the UK, usually trading as charities. Skateparks are used by people who ride skateboards, BMX and other bicycles and adapted wheelchairs called WCMX. They are also used by skaters and people who ride kick scooters. Skateparks have positive benefits for the physical and mental health of the people who use them. They are affordable and accessible to people of all ages, genders and cultures. Interest in skateparks has increased since the most recent Olympic Games when the UK won five medals in BMX and skateboard."},{"t":"p","x":"Flares Skatepark Flares Skatepark is a new indoor skatepark that has been open for two months. It is a registered charity and was founded by a local entrepreneur. The entrepreneur donated a large sum of money to buy and regenerate an old factory site into a place for the local community to enjoy. The area where Flares Skatepark is located has very few activities that younger people can enjoy. The skatepark was designed by a reputable skatepark designer and a team of employees and volunteers has been hired to run it. It is hoped that Flares Skatepark will become known as one of the best indoor skateparks in Europe."},{"t":"p","x":"The indoor skatepark is designed to target beginner, intermediate and experienced riders. It has a cafe offering hot drinks and snacks and customers can to hire equipment such as skateboards, BMX, scooters, helmets and safety pads. The skatepark was featured several times on local TV and in local newspapers which generated a lot of positive free publicity. A website was made for the skatepark to keep local residents informed. The website included an option to sign up to receive marketing information from the skatepark. As a result, Flares Skatepark already has a database of 2 000 email addresses with consent to receive marketing emails. The Flares Skatepark website allows customers to make advanced bookings online. This includes a section where customers can agree to receive marketing information. All riders complete a registration form and a parental consent form for under 18s. Increasing the customer base The management team of Flares Skatepark wants to increase the customer base within the next six months. They believe that they need to raise awareness that the skatepark is open, among both the local community and regular visitors to other skateparks. They want to invite professional skateboarders, BMX and scooter riders."},{"t":"p","x":"The management team of Flares Skatepark is considering two options to help achieve the goal of increasing the customer base."},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER Offering special deals for people in the local community."},{"t":"p","x":"OR Creating a full-day experience package to offer to professional and regular visitors to skateparks."},{"t":"p","x":"The management team of Flares Skatepark has allocated a budget of £10 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 6 months."},{"t":"p","x":"What is a skatepark?"},{"t":"p","x":"A place to skate or ride. It consists of ramps, bowls, pipes, banks, ledges, stairs, and rails. The surface can be either concrete or wood. It can be either indoors or outdoors."}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-2306","unit":2,"key":"2023-06","series":"June 2023","title":"Pet Majestic","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Pet Majestic. This should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Pet Majestic. The plan should include a:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Pet Majestic.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Pet Majestic. The business produces and sells pet accessories and treats. The business owners want to increase revenue."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for pet accessories in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research includes primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","media options."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"The pet food and accessory market in the UK Research carried out by the RSPCA has highlighted that pet ownership within the UK is growing year on year, with the largest growth being seen within city locations. 62% of households in the UK own at least one pet, with around two thirds of those owning two or more. Dogs continue to be the most popular household pet with 12.5 million in the UK. The UK market for pet food and accessories has seen rapid growth since the start of 2019. The market is now worth over £9.66 billion, with pet retailers now selling toys, food, fashion items and pet technology products. Research shows that Millennials and Gen-Z are the largest segment of new pet owners. Many researchers have suggested that this is in response to changes in social trends, such as working from home, and the value of pet ownership in supporting positive mental health. Pet retailer Pets at Home continues to see those in the 35–45 age bracket as being its primary demographic. The research company Statista has forecast an increase in spending on pet food and accessories of 4.5% per year. The average UK household will spend more than £2 500 per year on pets by 2025. A recent report from MORE TH>N pet insurance revealed that spending per household differs based on the pets they own. Pet owners spend on average £240 per month on a dog and £100 per month on a cat, purchasing food, treats and toys."},{"t":"p","x":"Global Market Insights forecasted that between 2022 to 2028, the pet technology market will grow by 20%. In 2021, The Times reported that a wide range of new tech-based pet products became available on the market such as:"},{"t":"li","x":["pet GPS tracker collar","interactive digital pet toys","smart pet bowls","heated pet beds","app-controlled pet doors","wearable pet cameras."]},{"t":"p","x":"However, there is a continued demand for more traditional products with toys, treats, collars and bedding accounting for 59% of the annual pet accessory spend in the UK. Premium brands offer individualised and bespoke pet food and products linked to nutritional balance and animal wellbeing. These products sell at higher prices. In addition, there has been a rapid growth in the number of discount retailers and supermarkets offering their own range of pet products for those with less disposable income."},{"t":"p","x":"Tails.com Tails.com is one of the leading online subscription and order services for dog food and treats. Republic of Cats offers a similar subscription for cats. Both businesses offer tailored feeding plans and pet treats, delivered directly to the customer each month. Over the last year, Tails.com’s customer numbers have grown by 10% and it has expanded its product portfolio to include seasonal and festive products alongside pet toys, which has helped to increase market share."},{"t":"p","x":"Tails.com initially focused on offering free trials to potential customers using flyers and direct mail. Its new approach to marketing includes increased use of social media alongside print-based marketing. To maintain customer loyalty, the business engages customers with an individualised approach where pet names appear on marketing materials."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Tails.com product range 2021–22","Product Average price","One month’s supply of dog food £33.00","100 g pack of dog treats £2.60","Soft dog toy £9.99","Rubber dog toy £8.99"]},{"t":"p","x":"Seasonal treat box (including soft pet toy, 2 x 100 g packs of dog treats and 2 x special edition food packs) £30.00 Good Boy Good Boy is a leading pet treat and accessory brand that predominantly caters for the dog market. It offers a range of treats, toys and accessories at competitive prices. These products are distributed through supermarkets, pet shops and discount retailers. In 2022, Good Boy achieved an £18 million growth in revenue, making it the fastest- growing pet treat brand on the market. Its marketing campaigns have historically used product placement and promotional advertising to drive sales. More recently, Good Boy has invested in TV advertising alongside an increased social media campaign. Good Boy’s most recent campaign combines social media, print-based and television advertising showing pets enjoying its range of treats in a variety of different settings. Its marketing message is aimed at reflecting the unconditional love that owners share with their pets."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Good Boy product range 2021–22","Product Average price","100 g pack of dog treats £2.00","Soft dog toy £5.00","Rubber dog toy £5.00"]},{"t":"p","x":"Seasonal treat box (including soft dog toy, rubber dog toy and 3 x 100 g packs of dog treats) £14.99 Pets at Home Pets at Home is considered the largest pet food and accessory retailer within the UK , selling just under £200 million pounds worth of products per year across its 457 stores and website. It stocks products from leading brands whilst also promoting and selling its own range of pet food, treats and accessories both online and in physical retail locations. During its growth stage, the retailer launched a loyalty scheme called the ‘VIP (Very Important Pets) Club’ . The scheme rewards customers with discount vouchers and special offers throughout the year. It also offers members a free monthly magazine that promotes its products and services. The loyalty scheme allows the business to gather important consumer information whilst also encouraging ongoing purchases. Its marketing message focuses on putting pets first, something that resonates within its brand image and throughout its marketing campaigns. In 2020, Pets at Home grew its VIP membership by 1.5 million members. The Pets at Home loyalty scheme now has over 10 million members nationwide. Pets at Home attributes the success of its loyalty scheme to personalising marketing communications to include the pet’s name, date of birth and breed."},{"t":"p","x":"As part of a new public relations initiative, Pets at Home has changed its approach to dealing with environmental issues by:"},{"t":"li","x":["increasing its use of recyclable materials across all stores","replacing plastic bags with paper alternatives","launching in-store recycling schemes for packaging","implementing digital mailouts to reduce the use of paper and cardboard."]},{"t":"p","x":"Similarly, it launched its ‘Lifelines’ programme, which donates money to pet charities and groups in response to the social trend of increasing charitable giving. The programme, which is linked to its ‘VIP Club’ , encourages customers to ‘do good’ by making in-store or online purchases, which then lead to contributions to their nominated charity. Members receive quarterly updates on how much they have raised."},{"t":"p","x":"Market trends and challenges Despite the ongoing growth within the pet sector, there are concerns for smaller retailers who are losing business to national brands. Forbes and Business Wire have forecast that the demand for pet accessories will peak in 2025, causing selling prices to decrease. The Times also highlights that smaller pet businesses may cease trading because of the predicted reduction in disposable income."},{"t":"p","x":"Reilly Roberts, a marketing specialist, highlights that, since June 2022, there has been a clear shift in customer demand within the pet industry. Customers are now moving from high-end premium products to mass-produced, low-cost products that are offered by bigger brands. Therefore, across the industry, there is a need for businesses to launch innovative product ranges and focus on the growth of the product portfolio, in order to simply maintain market share."},{"t":"p","x":"Research by the Pet Food Manufacturers Association suggests that the demand for sustainable, environmentally friendly pet food and pet products is only going to increase, with the main focus being on the pet market becoming more environmentally friendly."},{"t":"p","x":"Pet Majestic: market research report Month: April Sample size: 350 Location: Email survey"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Sample: Current customers of Pet Majestic Summary of questionnaire responses Gender:","c":["Male","Female","Other"],"v":["178","151","21"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age:","c":["16–24","25–32","33–41","42–50","51+"],"v":["62","106","84","39","59"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What pets do you have? (Select all that apply)","c":["Dog","Cat","Other"],"v":["297","161","52"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How often do you purchase pet products (food, treats or accessories)?","c":["More than once per week","Once per week","Once per month","Every few months"],"v":["143","44","155","8"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"On average, how much do you spend per month on pet food and treats?","c":["£0–£25","£26–55","£56–£75","£75+"],"v":["7","76","229","38"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"On average, how much do you spend per month on pet accessories?","c":["£0–£25","£26–55","£56–£75","£75+"],"v":["55","284","9","2"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Would you be interested in purchasing Pet Majestic-branded pet accessories in addition to food and treats?","c":["Yes","No","Unsure"],"v":["216","43","91"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which pet accessories would you be most interested in purchasing from Pet Majestic? (Select all that apply)","c":["Heated pet beds","Collars/Leads","Wearable pet camera","Toys","Clothing","Travel products","Grooming products","Smart pet bowl"],"v":["123","326","97","306","254","88","193","149"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What would encourage you to spend more per month with Pet Majestic? (Select all that apply)","c":["Increased product range","Improved quality","Improved pet technology","Lower prices","Increased offers","Faster delivery","Free samples","Loyalty programme","Celebrity endorsements"],"v":["289","241","181","111","183","32","68","203","95"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Pet Majestic has researched the following options for boosting sales: 1. TikTok ads"},{"t":"li","x":["TikTok has 100 million European users/community.","TikTok has 9.2 million active monthly users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 49 minutes a day. This is lower than the global average TikTok user, who spends 52 minutes actively using the app.","In 2022, 26% of TikTok users were aged 15–25 and 9.3% were 25–34 in the UK, accessing TikTok from smartphones.","8% of UK TikTok accounts are for pets, who can generate upwards of 75 000 views. 2. Y ouTube","Allows you to set a budget – daily budgets start from £6, depending on exposure needed.","More than 35.6 million adults in the UK use the platform.","35% of the UK population are active users of YouTube.","46% of women use YouTube in the UK vs 54% of men.","YouTube use is growing the fastest among Baby Boomers and Gen-X.","The most active demographic is aged 25–44, accounting for 44% of all users. 3. Instagram ads","Average Instagram ads cost between £0.35–£0.75 per click.","More than 30 million users in the UK, with 54% using the platform several times a day.","30% are aged 25–34 and 57% are women.","69% of users report posting about their pets on Instagram at least twice per week.","Fourth most-used platform in the UK in 2021–22.","46% of the UK population have an Instagram account. 4. Snapchat ads","Snapchat has 319 million people worldwide using the app.","Snapchat has 20.1 million active monthly users in the UK.","UK users spend around 31 minutes per day accessing content on Snapchat.","75% of users are 26–41 years of age.","62% of Snapchat users state that they post pet-related content at least three times per week.","Average cost for 10-second Snapchat clip and link is between £0.57–£1.32. 5. Google ads","Pay per click, with spend cap.","Monthly budget cap, which you can vary month by month.","Budget of £102 per month will generate estimated clicks or calls of between 140–235.","Google Ads allows you to do your own demographic targeting. For example, age, gender, household income, parental status. 6. Influencers","Cost – varies depending on influencer – between £50 and £500 per post.","Typically generates between 3–15K new followers per month depending on the influencer.","Most popular pet influencers in 2021 include: – Nala Cat (4.3 million followers) – Doug the Pug (3.9 million followers) – Juniper the Fox (3 million followers)."]},{"t":"p","x":"7. Billboards"},{"t":"li","x":["Billboard rental costs in the UK vary according to the size and location of the billboard. Typically, a standard billboard costs between £500–£600 for two weeks and smaller advertising panels such as bus-stop shelter advertisements cost between £240–£270 for two weeks. 8. Leaflets/Posters/Vouchers","There are two costs: the printing of the voucher and the distribution cost via newspapers inserts, magazine inserts or door-to-door leafleting.","Leaflets – A5 double-sided premium glossy 1 000 – £30.51, 5 000 for £65.62","Posters – A3 premium glossy 1 000 for £109.45.","Local newspaper insert: average of £30 per 1 000 leaflets.","National newspaper insert: ranges from £150 to £220 per 1 000 leaflets.","Magazine insert: ranges from £100 to £200 per 1 000 leaflets. 9. Newspapers/Magazines","Local newspapers – quarter-page colour advertisement from £250.","National newspapers – half-page advertisement – The Guardian – (2.9 million readership) – £11 000 – i – (1.2 million readership) – £5 950 – Metro – (2.3 million readership) – £23 900 (colour) – Daily Mail – (6.5 million readership) – £22 800 (colour).","My VIP (Pets at Home monthly free magazine – 500 000 readership) £1 000 for quarter page.","Your Dog / Your Cat (monthly magazine – 14 500 readership) £1 000 per full page. 10. Crufts Dog Show","Large-scale dog show held in the UK each year.","Over 131 000 visitors over a period of four days.","Televised with viewing figures of almost 8 million.","Trade stands were most visited part of the show in 2022.","83% of visitors own a dog.","A stand typically costs £295 per square metre, a small exhibition stand is likely to cost around £2 650. 11. Other","Staff to hand out leaflets/distribute posters – £10 per person per hour.","Video filming/production – £500–£10K.","Voice-over – from £500.","Motion graphics – from £3 000.","Stock images/video £500."]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Pet Majestic. This should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Pet Majestic."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include a:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Pet Majestic."},{"t":"p","x":"5 The pet food and accessory market in the UK Since 2020 the UK has seen a rapid rise in the number of households that have one or more pets. This has caused a significant growth in the UK pet food and accessory market. Pet food sales increased by over 150% with online sales of food and accessories exceeding £7.9 billion."},{"t":"p","x":"Average household spending on pet food and accessories has increased to £2 136 per year. This has led to the growth of new pet focused businesses in the UK. There has also been an increase in the number of brands that are launching pet accessories, such as Primark, Joules and PrettyLittleThing. Many UK supermarkets have also launched pet food or accessories to take advantage of this growth in the market. Businesses have expanded their product ranges of pet treats, accessories and pet technology as a way of improving sales and gaining market share. Pet food and accessories are sold both within retail stores and online. Since 2019, online sales have quadrupled. Many of the leading brands continue to use both distribution channels, but with the growth of subscription services in the pet product industry, many more are transferring to online only models."},{"t":"p","x":"The pet product industry faces challenges from animal welfare campaigners, including the RSPCA who suggest that pet food is one thing, but that accessories, clothing and pet technology can be quite stressful for animals. Leading brands are taking care that their businesses are not being linked to welfare or animal rights concerns. Many leading brands are launching animal welfare campaigns and charitable funds to lessen potential risks."},{"t":"p","x":"Pet Majestic Pet Majestic is an online business offering natural pet food and treats in the UK. The business has been operating since 2020 and has received several awards for its pet products. However, the business has recently experienced a decline in spend per customer which has concerned the owners."},{"t":"p","x":"The business uses its website and Facebook to promote its products and has managed to gain 2 500 followers since opening. Pet Majestic has built a small loyal customer base through its website and has over 500 customers who regularly order products. Research undertaken by Pet Majestic has highlighted that potential customers feel that the product range offered is too limited. The owners of the business have considered launching a new branded pet accessory range, and hope that this will improve brand recognition and increase sales."},{"t":"p","x":"Increasing sales The owners of Pet Majestic want to increase sales by launching a new range of branded pet accessories in addition to their current food and treat range. The business will have been open for three years in September and the owners hope to use the anniversary to launch the new products."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Pet Majestic are currently considering increasing their sales by: EITHER Increasing their online presence OR A print-based campaign including billboards and leaflets."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Pet Majestic have allocated a budget of £36 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 10 months."}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-2401","unit":2,"key":"2024-01","series":"January 2024","title":"Best Rest Hotels","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Best Rest Hotels. The rationale should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Best Rest Hotels. The plan should include:\n• a marketing mix\n• a marketing message\n• a selection of media\n• a campaign budget\n• a timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Best Rest Hotels.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Best Rest Hotels. The business owns and operates several budget hotels in the UK. The business owners want to increase market share."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the budget hotels market in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research includes primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","marketing mix","media options."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"The budget hotel market in the UK Statista reports that in 2023 the UK hotel market was estimated to be worth £19.8 billion. Budget hotels make up 59% of this market. The budget hotel market is forecast to grow by 17% in the next five years. IBISWorld suggests that the number of budget hotels will reach 7 500 by 2028."},{"t":"p","x":"The demand for hotel accommodation has increased rapidly throughout the UK. The cost of hotel rooms has increased, and cheaper options are in higher demand. BoutiqueHotelier.com reports an increase in customers moving from boutique and specialist hotels to the budget market. This change has caused significant pricing battles between competitors. MarketingWeek also suggests that the demand for budget hotels will grow very fast."},{"t":"p","x":"The growth of the budget hotel market has led to European brand B&B Hotels announcing that it will open 100 new hotels across the UK by 2035. These hotels will offer customers low-cost, high-quality accommodation. The brand has reduced costs by using self-service check-in technology. They no longer provide ‘free’ items such as toiletries, tea, coffee and Wi-Fi."},{"t":"p","x":"Mintel suggests that budget hotels should change their focus to leisure customers rather than corporate and business. CoStar, a commercial information company, highlights that consumer trends have changed to focus on leisure opportunities such as golfing, spa breaks and countryside walks rather than business events, conferences and meetings. This change has led to more bookings for budget hotels in rural locations, reducing the demand for city centre accommodation."},{"t":"p","x":"There are five leading brands in the budget hotel market – Premier Inn, Travelodge, Holiday Inn Express, ibis Budget and Hampton by Hilton. Before 2020, smaller independent businesses provided the most accommodation in the budget hotel market. However, since 2020, economic and political challenges have led to many smaller, independent hotels closing."},{"t":"p","x":"Premier Inn Premier Inn is the largest hotel brand in the UK. It has more than 77 000 rooms available in over 841 hotels. The business continues to grow year-on-year and aims to increase its share of the budget hotel market by another 17% by 2025. However, operating costs are increasing at a rate which may lead to hotel closures over the next two years. The business focuses on a wide target market, including individuals, couples, families and business customers. Therefore, it relied on television advertising in the past. In 2022, it launched the ‘Rest Easy’ campaign to broaden its customer base and improve its market share. The campaign used character stories showing different groups/individuals using hotel rooms. The marketing team behind the campaign explained that the focus was on emphasising the familiar feeling, consistency and quality of Premier Inn. The campaign was a combination of 60-second television adverts with social media, print and radio advertising."},{"t":"p","x":"Premier Inn – 2023 average room rates Room Type Description Average rate per night Standard room Luxury king or single size beds, ensuite shower, TV with Freeview, blackout curtains, white towels, desk area, tea and coffee facilities and free Wi-Fi. £65.00 Premier plus room Luxury king size beds, ensuite, rainfall shower, TV with Freeview, relaxing LED mood lighting, blackout curtains, premium coffee machine, complimentary luxury toiletries, sweet treats, water and free ultimate Wi-Fi. £87.00 Extras Premier Inn Breakfast charged at £9.99 per person."},{"t":"p","x":"They also offer a ‘pillow menu’ so customers can select what type of pillows they have."},{"t":"p","x":"Travelodge Travelodge currently operates over 590 hotels across the UK and Ireland and is the second largest hotel brand in the country. The brand was forced to close 38 of their hotels between 2020 and 2021 because of low occupancy and large reductions in bookings. In May 2022, Travelodge began a programme of redesigning 50 hotels after a survey of 5 000 UK business and leisure travellers. The people surveyed said that style, choice and quality were priorities when booking. The new rooms maintain the signature navy blue brand colour but include additional colours, sustainable fabrics, motion sensor lighting and shades to promote their marketing message of luxury relaxation. The hotels also feature a contemporary restaurant and a bar called The Bar Café. Last year, Travelodge launched a new marketing campaign with the aim of engaging with customers and improving growth. The business used a combination of social media, pop-up adverts and video advertising. It also launched a sales promotion, which targeted seasonal customers and offered rooms for less than £10 per night per person. Travelodge – 2023 average room rates Room Type Description Average rate per night Standard room Comfy king size beds, ensuite shower, TV with Freeview, white towels, desk area, tea and coffee facilities and 30 minutes free Wi-Fi. £75.00 Super room Comfy king size beds, ensuite rainfall shower and handheld shower, 32” TV with Freeview, relaxing LED mood lighting, blackout curtains, premium coffee machine and free Wi-Fi."},{"t":"tbl","x":["£101.00","Extras","Wi-Fi is charged at £3.00 for 24 hours for standard rooms.","Unlimited breakfast is also available in most hotels for £8.99","Take-out breakfast is available in most hotels for £5.50","‘Going Green’"]},{"t":"p","x":"McKinsey reported that 76% of customers are now requiring more environmentally friendly accommodation. The report shows that customers are now more likely to pay higher nightly rates for a more environmentally friendly hotel and will prioritise booking at hotels that use recycled materials or green energy."},{"t":"p","x":"Travelodge uses recycled and sustainable materials throughout its new hotels. For example, the carpets are made from recycled fishing nets and plastic bottles. The business has also set out its long-term goal to be environmentally friendly. It has installed low energy heating systems, air conditioning, lighting and solar power. Holiday Inn Express has responded to demands for sustainability by launching its ‘Green Engage System’ . The system ensures that all Holiday Inn Express hotels use sustainable materials, environmentally friendly cleaning and hygiene products, and that they improve energy efficiency."},{"t":"p","x":"Low Occupancy Budget hotels often measure their success based on the number of rooms sold (occupancy). In 2019, budget hotels throughout the UK were reporting high occupancy levels. Premier Inn and Travelodge were both achieving 83% occupancy in London, and 76% occupancy across the rest of the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"However, the last three years have been challenging for the budget hotel sector. Statista recorded a drop in occupancy levels to 22% in April 2020 a new, all-time low for the sector. 2021 was not much of an improvement with the first half of the year showing occupancy at well below 50%. Despite improvement in occupancy levels in 2023, budget hotels continue to struggle, and are having to find new ways of targeting and attracting customers."},{"t":"p","x":"Best Rest Hotels: Market Research Report Month: October Sample size: 1 800 Location: The research was collected through a social media survey. Sample: Individuals between the ages of 18 and 42 Summary of questionnaire responses Gender:"},{"t":"p","x":"Male Female Other 1 003 654 143"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age:","c":["18–22","23–27","28–32","33–37","38–42"],"v":["124","351","638","203","484"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Have you stayed in a UK-based hotel in the past twelve months? Yes No 1 322 478"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"If yes, what was the main purpose for your hotel stay?","c":["Leisure (Solo)","Leisure (Couple)","Leisure (Family)","Business (Solo)","Business (Group)","Other"],"v":["113","549","376","189","63","32"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following budget hotel brands have you heard of? (Select all that apply) Travelodge Premier Inn Holiday Inn Express Hampton by Hilton 1 792 1 732 1 490 634 ibis Best Western Britannia Best Rest Hotels 743 808 231 388"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What is the average price you would expect to pay for a night at a budget hotel in the UK?","c":["£30–44","£45–59","£60–74","£75–89","£90+"],"v":["266","419","404","528","183"]}},{"t":"p","x":"When looking for a budget hotel, what factors are important to you? (Select all that apply) Price Cleanliness Location Facilities 1 603 1 735 447 796 Food and drink offer Wi-Fi access Guest reviews Being green 743 1 646 884 409 “Best Rest Hotels are an exciting budget hotel brand offering basic rooms at low prices throughout the UK. Each room offers ensuite facilities, double or twin beds and Wi-Fi access. ” Based on the description of the rooms provided by Best Rest Hotels, would you consider booking one of their hotel rooms for your next trip? Yes No 1 398 402"},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Best Rest Hotels has researched the following media options for its upcoming marketing campaign: 1 Leaflets/Posters/Vouchers"},{"t":"li","x":["There are two costs: the printing of the voucher and the distribution cost via newspapers inserts, magazine inserts or door-to-door leafleting.","Leaflets – A5 double-sided premium glossy 1 000–£30.51, 5 000–£65.62","Posters – A3 premium glossy 1 000–£109.45","Local newspaper insert: average of £30 per 1 000 leaflets.","National newspaper insert: ranges from £150 to £220 per 1 000 leaflets.","Magazine insert: ranges from £100 to £200 per 1 000 leaflets. 2 Newspapers and Magazine","Local newspapers – quarter-page colour advertisement from £250","National newspapers – half-page colour advertisement","The Guardian (2.9 million readership) – £11 000","i newspaper (1.2 million readership) – £5 950","Metro (2.3 million readership) – £23 900 (colour)","Travel magazines – full-page colour advertisement","Group Leisure and Travel Magazine (97 000 readership) – £2 640","Business Travel Magazine (15 000 readership) – £1 680 3 TikTok ads","Tik Tok has 100 million European users/community.","TikTok has 9.2 million active monthly users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 49 minutes a day. The global average TikTok user spends 52 minutes per day actively using the app.","In 2022, 26% of TikTok users in the UK were aged 15–25 and 9.3% were 25–34, accessing TikTok from smartphones.","Average cost: £50 per day, minimum spend £500 per campaign."]},{"t":"p","x":"4 Y ouTube"},{"t":"li","x":["Allows you to set a budget – daily budgets start from £6, depending on exposure needed.","More than 35.6 million adults in the UK use the platform.","35% of the UK population are active users of YouTube.","In the UK 46% of women and 54% of men use YouTube.","YouTube use is growing the fastest amongst Baby Boomers and Gen-X.","The most active demographic is aged 25–44, accounting for 44% of all users. 5 Instagram ads","Average Instagram ads cost between £0.35–£0.75 per click.","More than 30 million users in the UK with 54% using the platform several times per day.","45% of travellers use Instagram to choose budget hotels and locations.","41 million posts include the hashtag #hotel and 80K posts include #budgethotel.","Fourth most-used platform in the UK in 2021–22.","46% of the UK population have an Instagram account. 6 Google Ads","Pay per click, with spend cap.","Monthly budget cap, which you can vary month by month.","Budget of £102 per month will generate estimated clicks or calls of 140–235.","Google Ads allows you to do your own demographic targeting, e.g. age, gender, household income, parental status. 7 Influencers","Cost – varies depending on influencer – between £50 and £500 per post.","Typically generates between 3–15K new followers per month depending on the influencer.","Some of the most popular travel bloggers in 2022 include:","A Lady in London (173K followers)","Hand Luggage Only (180K followers)","Travel Beans (281K Followers)."]},{"t":"p","x":"8 Television advertising"},{"t":"li","x":["There are two costs for television advertising: the production cost and the advertising time.","Video filming/production can cost between £500 and £10K depending on the length of the video.","Daytime slots on ITV are about £3 700 to £4 700 for a 60-second advert.","Daytime slots on Channel 4 are about £1 100 to £2 200 for a 60-second advert.","Evening slots on different channels can vary between £10 000 and £20 000. 9 Billboards","Billboard rental costs in the UK vary according to the size and location of the billboard.","A standard billboard costs £500–£600 for two weeks.","Smaller advertising panels like bus-stop shelter advertisements cost between £240–£270 for two weeks. 10 Other","Staff to give leaflets/distribute posters – £11 per person per hour.","Postage and distribution costs for leaflets/flyers – £120 per 1 000.","Voice-over – from £650.","Motion graphics – from £3 000.","Stock images/video £600."]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Best Rest Hotels. The rationale should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Best Rest Hotels."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["a marketing mix","a marketing message","a selection of media","a campaign budget","a timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Best Rest Hotels."},{"t":"p","x":"5 The market for budget hotels in the UK Before 2023, the budget hotel market in the UK was made up of smaller independent businesses. However, more recently there has been increased growth from branded hotel chains and a reduction in the number of new, independent hotels. The budget hotel market has grown steadily over the last ten years. There has been a significant increase in the number of budget hotels opening in city centre locations. However, the market has suffered because of the global pandemic that caused large numbers of independent hotels to close. Budget hotel chains have recovered well and many chains are now reporting increased bookings."},{"t":"p","x":"There are currently five leading brands in the UK budget hotel market – Premier Inn, Travelodge, Holiday Inn Express, ibis Budget and Hampton by Hilton. Premier Inn and Travelodge are both recognised as the most popular budget hotel chains in the UK market. Budget brands tend to target a wide audience of potential customers, with business customers generating almost 65% of a budget hotel’s income. For this reason, all five leading brands use ‘business booker’ systems to target business customers and the corporate market."},{"t":"p","x":"A potential threat to the budget hotel market has been the development of online platforms allowing people to rent out their spare rooms or properties. One example of these platforms is Airbnb, which has grown in popularity. In 2021, Airbnb reported that there were 18.9 million bookings for UK properties. Most listings on Airbnb are for private homes. Small independent hotels can also list rooms on Airbnb. Best Rest Hotels Best Rest Hotels is a chain of budget hotels in the UK. The business has been operating for five years and now has 32 hotels throughout the North of England and Wales. Each hotel is located near a large town or a city with good transport links. The hotels offer a very basic level of service to customers. Rooms are booked online using the company’s own website and customers check-in using a computer in the reception area. The rooms provide customers with basic facilities including Wi-Fi access, double or single beds and towels. Providing basic facilities helps a business to keep costs low and maximise profits."},{"t":"p","x":"The hotels do not have bars or restaurants but instead provide vending machines in reception areas where customers can buy snacks and drinks. The hotels are normally located near pubs and restaurants where customers can buy meals. Best Rest Hotels currently target business customers who travel to attend meetings. Now, about 60% of all bookings for rooms are made by leisure customers and the remaining 40% of bookings are made by business customers."},{"t":"p","x":"Over the past two years, the business has experienced a rapid loss of bookings but now bookings are starting to improve. The business reported occupancy levels of only 45% in 2023 which was significantly below the national average. However, the business has now started to improve. By checking booking information, the owners have found that families and couples are now more likely to book a room at one of the hotels rather than business customers. This change is helping to increase occupancy."},{"t":"p","x":"Increasing market share Most of the hotels owned by Best Rest Hotels are reporting low occupancy levels and reduced bookings. The owners are keen to improve the market share and increase the number of bookings across all their hotels within the next 12 months. They are considering developing their promotional activity to include: EITHER Using influencers to persuade new leisure customers to make regular bookings OR Launching a digital and print-based campaign to target business customers, travel agents and large businesses."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Best Rest Hotels have allocated a budget of £180 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 12 months."}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-2406","unit":2,"key":"2024-06","series":"June 2024","title":"Funky Flasks — reusable drinkware","theme":"","source":"pearson","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Funky Flasks. The rationale should include:\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Funky Flasks. The plan should include a:\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Funky Flasks.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Funky Flasks. The business produces and sells reusable drinkware. The business owners want to increase sales."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for reusable drinkware in the UK."},{"t":"p","x":"This research includes primary and secondary data relating to:"},{"t":"li","x":["competitors","market size, share and structure","target market","trends","external influences","media options."]},{"t":"p","x":"You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"p","x":"The reusable drinkware market in the UK Before 2013, single-use drinkware products such as plastic water bottles and disposable coffee cups were widely used by consumers. However, over the past 10 years there has been a significant change in the way consumers view and use drinkware products. Reusable options now being the popular choice."},{"t":"p","x":"The 2017 BBC documentary Blue Planet II raised concerns over the level of plastics found in the oceans, the environment and the global food chain. The documentary made people aware of the impact of single-use drinkware products. The same year, The Guardian and Science Focus both highlighted a significant increase in the number of people who were reducing their use of single-use water bottles and cups. Luxurious Magazine stated that 75% of consumers in the UK were now more conscious of their personal impact on the environment."},{"t":"p","x":"The global emphasis on climate change and the reduction of single-use plastics is a driving force for the growth in the reusable drinkware market. The reusable drinkware market is globally one of the fastest growing sectors, with the value expected to reach approximately $12.6 billion by 2030. Research by The Guardian highlighted that only 20% of people in the UK used a reusable bottle or cup on a regular basis in 2015. In 2023, this use had increased to 59% of people, with an expectation that by 2028, 80% of people in the UK will have and use a reusable drinkware product. Other initiatives have strengthened the increase in sales of reusable drinkware and recyclable, cardboard drinkware products:"},{"t":"li","x":["the UK Government banned many single-use plastics from October 2023","many local councils, transport hubs, event venues, schools and colleges have installed a network of water fountains for people to refill a water bottle","the Refill app informs people where they can refill both water bottles and reusable coffee cups."]},{"t":"p","x":"The reusable drinkware market is dominated by big brands, including S’well, Chilly’s, YETI, Hydro Flask and Ocean Bottle. Recently, there has been increased competition from independent and small-scale businesses operating online and in pop-up locations across the country. The pricing of reusable drinkware can vary from £2 to over £100 per item. Large brands have also contributed to the success of the reusable drinkware market, with Starbucks, Caffé Nero and M&S being some of the retailers to offer discounts for using reusable drinkware. However, Will de Freitas, an environment journalist, suggests that it is not the discount that leads people to purchase reusable drinkware but instead the fact that it is becoming a trend to carry, use and reuse drinkware. The demand for reusable drinkware products has led to many businesses, colleges and charities providing customers with free, branded bottles or cups. The demand for reusable drinkware has contributed significantly to national and international growth of the market. Smaller, independent producers have suffered losses in revenue and market share. These losses have been linked to consumers and corporate clients moving to larger brands which can offer cheaper, bespoke products."},{"t":"p","x":"Despite the forecasted growth, a report by the HuffPost highlighted that growth in reusable drinkware may be temporary. When a consumer has a bottle/cup, there is limited need to repeat purchase unless it is lost or damaged. Similarly, Talking Retail found that large numbers of drink companies are now changing from single-use plastic to reusable glass bottles. This change targets eco-friendly consumers and challenges producers of cheaper products."},{"t":"p","x":"Chilly’s Chilly’s was launched in 2010 by a university student in the UK. The business is now one of the largest reusable drinkware brands in the UK with annual sales of over £44 million. The business now collaborates with various artists, designers and brands to create unique and limited-edition products that are released on a regular basis and sold at a premium price."},{"t":"p","x":"The business realised there was a growing trend and demand for reusability, so it extended its product range to include reusable coffee cups, straws, food pots and more recently a redesigned sports bottle targeted at Millennials/GenY. The business now supplies products to customers across the world, and research by Statista suggests Chilly’s sales will double in the next two years."},{"t":"p","x":"Chilly’s Product Range 2022/2023 Product Description Average Price Metal bottles The Original/Series 2 with screw-top lid for hot and cold drinks."},{"t":"p","x":"Original Sports with sports cap lid for cold drinks."},{"t":"p","x":"Series 2 Flip with flip lid and steel straw."},{"t":"p","x":"Available in a range of patterns, colours and sizes."},{"t":"tbl","x":["Standard £26.00","Limited edition £30.00","Standard £26.00","Limited edition £30.00","£38.00","Metal cup Original Cup/Series 2 with a sip-lid for hot drinks."]},{"t":"p","x":"Available in a range of patterns, colours and sizes. £20.00 to £36.00 Accessories Different types and colours of lids, reusable straws and bottle bags. £3.00 to £50.00 Extras Personalised engraving on the bottle or cup £10.00 To boost the brand awareness, Chilly’s launched a pop-up shop in London for four days in November 2022. The pop-up shop allowed potential customers to see the product range in person. The pop-up shop contributed to an increase in sales revenue and social interactions through hashtag #ChillysPopUp."},{"t":"p","x":"In May 2023, Chilly’s launched its new range of reusable bottles, the Series 2 Flip. The new product was aimed directly at a younger market, and so the marketing strategy had to match. To promote the product Chilly’s opened a unique, immersive experience, which included a series of rooms linked to each of the colours of the new product. There were photo opportunities, hashtags and social media using the tagline ‘Flip the Ordinary’ . The campaign was a huge success, increasing website visitors by 26% and social media interactions by over 300%."},{"t":"p","x":"S’well S’well was launched in 2010 with a single range of reusable water bottles. Now it has a wide range of reusable drinkware including travel mugs and reusable straws. The brand focuses on providing sleek and stylish products that are both beautiful and eco-friendly. The business has a strong focus on giving back to communities in need and has a longstanding partnership with water.org. S’well makes a donation to water.org from each product sold. Water.org helps provide safe and clean water to families in need around the world."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2017 S’well had a 48% growth in followers on Instagram, which contributed to its overall success and increased brand recognition tenfold. The business continues to forecast growth year-on-year, and has strong links with influencers and celebrities who help to promote the brand. S’well also regularly launches new product designs, styles and themes to maintain interest and target new segments of the market. S’well Product Range 2022/2023 Product Description Price Metal bottle Bottle with screw-top lid for hot and cold drinks. Available in a range of patterns, colours and sizes."},{"t":"p","x":"Standard £29.99 Limited edition £36.99 Traveller Travel-size metal bottle with a screw-top lid for hot and cold drinks."},{"t":"p","x":"Available in a range of patterns, colours and sizes."},{"t":"p","x":"Standard £22.99 Limited edition £32.99 Mug Metal, insulated mug with sliding lid."},{"t":"p","x":"Available in a range of patterns, colours and sizes. £27.99 to £36.99 Extras Personalised printing on the bottle or mug £10.00 At first, the brand relied on word-of-mouth marketing, alongside some basic social media interactions to promote and increase sales. Its immediate success on Instagram led to the business launching a series of social media campaigns that focused on showcasing the design and look of the products."},{"t":"p","x":"In 2020, S’well launched its first brand campaign to inspire consumers to find more ways to use less plastic. The message of the campaign was sustainability, which was reflected in all aspects of the digital campaign, products and website. The campaign focused predominantly on Instagram through posts, stories and hashtags with links to the S’well website and ecommerce pages. The business also launched its campaign at London Fashion Week to target large audiences and align products to the fashionable and trendy consumer."},{"t":"p","x":"Josh Dean, previous Chief Marketing Officer of S’well, stated that the most important part of the business and brand is the value of sustainability and being environmentally friendly. All aspects of packaging, printing and production in the UK are 100% recyclable. The brand maintains this message throughout all of its campaigns. Consumer Demand The target market is broad, with products aimed at children, teens, adults, sports enthusiasts and those with niche interests. Businesses are now collaborating with music, sports and film brands. With such a large target market, the demand for products is constantly changing. Reports by The Atlantic highlight that consumers want variety, colour, design and style. Millennials now see reusable drinkware as fashion accessories to show that they are ‘green’ , trendy and up to date. Businesses are also offering products with technology that records consumption, prompts users to drink and connects to mobile devices to record health data."},{"t":"p","x":"Funky Flasks: Market Research Report Month: April 2024 Sample size: 750 Location: Busy shopping centre with gym"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Sample: Individuals between the ages of 16 and 75 Summary of questionnaire responses Gender:","c":["Male","Female","Other"],"v":["403","341","6"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age:","c":["16–25","26–35","36–45","46–55","56–65","66–75"],"v":["188","153","133","96","81","99"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you currently own a reusable drinkware product?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["363","387"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you currently own more than one reusable drinkware product?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["685","65"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Why would you buy a reusable drinkware product? (Select all that apply.)","c":["To reduce my use of single-use products","To save money by having something I can refill","To keep up to date with trends","To support a brand or business","To increase my water intake each day (health reasons)"],"v":["274","198","402","27","151"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"When would you use a reusable drinkware product?","c":["When commuting to work/college","When attending an event","When taking part in sports","When on holiday","When socialising"],"v":["140","174","359","42","35"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What price would you pay for a high-quality reusable drinkware product?","c":["£0–5","£5–10","£10–15","£15–20","£20+"],"v":["85","106","372","104","83"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"When selecting a reusable drinkware product, what are the most important features? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Range of colours/designs","Branding","Size","Celebrity endorsement","Technology","Durability","Leak proof","Ability to customise","Time drinks stay hot/cold"],"v":["721","225","687","197","466","143","405","503","598"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Would you be happy to pay extra for your reusable drinkware product to be personalised with an image or name?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["694","56"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where would you be likely to purchase a reusable drinkware product? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Online","Pop-up location","Event/festival","Supermarket/ retailers","Sports club/gym"],"v":["693","288","371","704","109"]}},{"t":"p","x":"Research on media selection Funky Flasks has researched the following media options for its upcoming marketing campaign: 1. Leaflets/Posters/Vouchers"},{"t":"li","x":["There are two costs: the printing of the voucher and the distribution cost via newspaper inserts, magazine inserts or door-to-door leafleting.","Leaflets – A5 double-sided premium glossy 1 000 for £30.51, 5 000 for £65.62.","Posters – A3 premium glossy 1 000 for £109.45.","Local newspaper insert: average of £30 per 1 000 leaflets.","National newspaper insert: ranges from £150 to £220 per 1 000 leaflets.","Magazine insert: ranges from £100 to £200 per 1 000 leaflets. 2. TikTok ads","TikTok has 100 million European users/community.","TikTok has 9.2 million active monthly users in the UK who engage with the app for an average of 49 minutes a day. This is lower than the global average TikTok user, who spends 52 minutes actively using the app.","In 2022, 26% of TikTok users in the UK were aged 15–25 and 9.3% were 25–34, accessing TikTok from smartphones.","Paid adverts cost around £10 for 1 000 views. 3. Instagram ads","Average Instagram ads cost £0.35–£0.75 per click.","More than 30 million users in the UK, with 54% using the platform several times a day.","Fourth most-used platform in the UK in 2021–22.","46% of the UK population have an Instagram account. 4. Influencers","Cost – varies depending on influencer – between £50 and £500 per post.","Typically generates between 3–15K new followers per month depending on the influencer.","Influencers are linked to specific areas of interest or locality, which allows careful targeting.","Some influencers will post for free if they are given new products, collaborations or samples. 5. Festivals/Trade Shows","Small, local festivals/events – £10–£50 per stall/stand.","Medium festivals and events £100–£1 250 per stall/stand.","Large festivals and events – £250–£2 000 per stall/stand.","National trade show – £1 800 for 9m 2 stand.","This is a way of engaging with potential customers while showcasing your product and offering free samples.","Creates brand awareness and showcases products to customers, allows for face-to-face sales.","Trade shows allow for bulk orders and wholesale ordering or collaborations."]},{"t":"p","x":"6. Sponsorship"},{"t":"li","x":["Sponsorship of a sports team or event allows for promotion to large target audiences who have a common interest.","Packages can be negotiated with different groups/organisations and could be a paid sponsorship or could be through the provision of products.","Average paid for sponsorship ranging between £200–£1 000.","National sports teams £500–£2 500.","Providing products is not free. There is a cost to the business. 7. Television advertising","There are two costs: production of the advert and buying advertisement slots.","Video filming/production can cost between £500 and £10 000, depending on the length of the video.","Daytime slots on ITV are about £3 700 to £4 700 for a 60-second advert.","Daytime slots on Channel 4 are around £1 100 to £2 200 for a 60-second advert.","Evening slots on different channels can vary between £10 000 and £20 000. 8. Billboards","Billboard rental costs in the UK vary according to the size and location of the billboard.","Typically, a standard billboard costs £500–£600 for two weeks and smaller advertising panels like bus stop shelter advertisements cost £240–£270 for two weeks. 9. Other","Staff to hand out leaflets/distribute posters – £10 per person per hour.","Postage and distribution costs for leaflets/flyers – £120 per 1 000.","Voice-over – from £500.","Motion graphics – from £3 000.","Stock images/video £500."]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task"},{"t":"p","x":"You must complete BOTH activities."},{"t":"p","x":"You will need to refer to the additional task information on the following pages and your notes from the research and preparatory work undertaken in Part A."},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 1"},{"t":"p","x":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Funky Flasks. The rationale should include:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing aims and objectives","analysis of research data on the market and competition","an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided","a justification for your rationale. (Total for Activity 1 = 34 marks)"]},{"t":"h","x":"Activity 2"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Funky Flasks."},{"t":"p","x":"The plan should include a:"},{"t":"li","x":["marketing mix","marketing message","selection of media","campaign budget","timescale."]},{"t":"p","x":"You must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Funky Flasks."},{"t":"p","x":"5 The market for reusable drinkware in the UK The past seven years have seen a rapid increase in the demand for reusable drinkware. Historically, customers were using cardboard cups, plastic bottles and single-use drinkware that was readily available. However, the spotlight is now on environmental sustainability and the need to protect the planet. This has led to a rapid increase in the demand for reusable glass and metal drinkware."},{"t":"p","x":"In the past year reusable drinkware products have become fashion accessories and consequently there is a demand for personalisation, vibrant colours and unique designs. Brands like Chilly’s and Hydro Flask have adapted their product ranges to include engraved names on bottles, with S’well and Chilly’s both offering limited edition designs and colours. In addition, many large-scale events, venues and festivals are working in collaboration with drinkware producers to design and sell their own branded reusable drinkware. Glogg is a UK brand that works with Glastonbury to produce 200 000 stainless steel pint cups for the festival each year. This collaboration gives Glogg a huge boost in revenue and brand recognition."},{"t":"p","x":"GlobeNewswire reported that the reusable drinkware market is predicted to grow until it reaches its peak value of around $13 billion in 2032. The growth of the market brings with it increased competition, leading to an estimated 15 000 new reusable drinkware businesses opening by 2030. In contrast, Future Market Insights suggests that consumer demand and trends change so frequently that reusable drinkware could be seen as unfashionable by 2026."},{"t":"p","x":"However, there are multiple campaigns throughout the UK that encourage people to use reusable drinkware. Some food and drink businesses offer free refills or discounts if customers use a reusable cup or bottle. WaterUK is the trailblazer in the campaign to encourage switching to reusable drinkware. It recently launched Refill , the UK’s leading app which connects people to free places to refill their water. Funky Flasks Funky Flasks is a medium-sized online reusable drinkware business in the UK. The business specialises in producing and selling reusable drinkware and offers a 500 ml bottle in a variety of styles and colours. Recently, the business increased its product range and now offers customisable drinkware at a premium price. To support business growth, Funky Flasks promoted products using a stand at festivals and events, which proved successful. In the past two years, the business has launched a social media page on TikTok, which has helped it to gain 5 000 followers. The business also has a database of 1 200 customers, who receive regular newsletters and sales promotions via email."},{"t":"p","x":"Funky Flasks sells its products through its website. Potential customers can browse the different designs available and request personalisation. Products are then shipped direct to the customer via post. The whole product range is made from high quality stainless steel and is free from plastic. Products are manufactured in the UK and the business donates 10% from each sale to national animal and environmental charities. The owners of the business are also keen to link with local artists and designers to help create limited edition product ranges to help raise awareness of environmental issues."},{"t":"p","x":"Increasing sales Over the past 12 months Funky Flasks reported reductions in the sales of its main product range, despite a small increase in purchases of customised drinkware. The owners are worried that they are no longer reaching enough potential customers and want to quickly boost sales."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Funky Flasks are considering two options to help them achieve their goal: EITHER Exploring sponsorship of events and festivals OR Using supermarkets and retailers to sell their products."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Funky Flasks have allocated a budget of £60 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for six months."}],"prep":{"pack":true,"sides":"two"}},{"id":"U2-R1","unit":2,"key":"9901","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Glow Candle Co. — hand-poured candles","theme":"Gifting season campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Glow Candle Co..\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Glow Candle Co..\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Glow Candle Co..","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Glow Candle Co. The business produces and sells hand-poured soy candles. The business owners want to increase sales in the autumn and winter gifting season."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for candles in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK candle market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK home fragrance market, which includes candles, is estimated to be worth about £1.2 billion and has grown by an average of 6% a year over the past five years. Candles are bought both for the home and as gifts: Mintel reports that 41% of all candle purchases are gifts and that sales peak between October and December. Interest in sustainable products has led to growth in soy and vegan wax candles, refill pots and recyclable packaging. A Which? survey in 2023 found that 62% of UK buyers say they would pay more for a candle that is plastic-free and made in the UK. Social media “candle hauls”, unboxing videos and TikTok recommendations have made candles a visual, shareable product. The market is forecast to grow to about £1.6 billion by 2028."},{"t":"p","x":"However, a trade-journal article warns that the cost of living is leading many consumers to buy cheaper supermarket candles, and that rising wax and shipping costs are reducing the margins of small producers. Candle-related fires have also led to calls for clearer safety labelling."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Brand","Position in the market","Typical price (per 200 g candle)","Main promotion"],"rows":[["Luma & Co","Premium, luxury scents; strong online brand","£28.00","Influencers, magazine features, own stores"],["HomeBright","Value range sold in supermarkets","£8.00","Supermarket offers, TV advertising"],["Wick & Willow","Independent, vegan wax, eco packaging","£18.00","Instagram, craft fairs"],["Glow Candle Co.","Hand-poured soy, UK-made, plastic-free packaging","£16.00 (limited editions £22.00)","Own website, Instagram (4,200 followers), email list of 1,500"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Luma & Co has an estimated 18% share of UK online candle sales. In 2023 it opened a pop-up “scent bar” in Manchester for two weeks, which increased its website traffic by 34% and social media mentions by 210%. Wick & Willow offers refill pots at £11 and a loyalty card; its sales grew by 15% last year. HomeBright dominates supermarket shelves with seasonal gift sets."},{"t":"h","x":"Glow Candle Co."},{"t":"p","x":"Glow Candle Co. was founded four years ago by two friends who pour candles in a converted garage workshop. The business sells through its website and occasional craft fairs. Over the past 12 months sales of its core range (six scents) fell by 8%, although sales of personalised-label candles increased by 12%. The owners donate 5% of profits to a local woodland charity. A shortage of storage space limits how many candles can be made before the Christmas season. The owners want to boost sales over the autumn and winter and have set a budget for the campaign."},{"t":"h","x":"Glow Candle Co.: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: September 2024 · Sample size: 600 · Location: busy shopping centre, outside a gift shop · Sample: individuals aged 16 to 75"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["16–25","26–35","36–45","46–55","56–65","66–75"],"v":["92","138","126","104","81","59"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy candles at least twice a year?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["408","192"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy candles as gifts?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["372","228"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What do you consider most important when buying a candle? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Scent","Price","Burn time","Eco-friendly/plastic-free","Packaging/design","Personalisation","Brand"],"v":["551","438","301","276","334","215","172"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a good-quality candle?","c":["£0–8","£8–12","£12–16","£16–20","£20+"],"v":["74","138","214","121","53"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where would you most likely buy a candle as a gift? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Online","Gift shops","Christmas markets","Supermarkets","Department stores"],"v":["356","412","241","307","188"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which social media platforms do you use daily? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Instagram","TikTok","Facebook","Pinterest","None"],"v":["342","264","288","97","51"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Glow Candle Co. has researched the following media options for its autumn/winter campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Instagram and Facebook ads","£0.40–£0.90 per click; 35 million UK users; targeting by age, interest and location"],["TikTok ads","About £9 per 1,000 views; 26% of UK users are aged 15–24"],["Influencers","£30–£400 per post depending on followers; some work for free products; typically gain 2–10K new followers a month"],["Christmas markets / pop-up stands","£150–£900 per weekend stand (large city markets £600–£900); staff £11 per hour; footfall 5,000–30,000"],["Gift-shop and retailer listings","Listing fee £250 per retailer; retailer takes 35% of the selling price; 120 independent gift shops identified"],["Local radio","£200–£1,200 per week for a 30-second advert; production £350"],["Printing","A5 leaflets £28 per 1,000; posters A3 £95 per 100; local newspaper insert £30 per 1,000"],["Other","Photography £400 · video production £800–£4,000 · packaging redesign £1,500 · gift-box samples £3 each · website upgrade £2,200 · insurance for stands £120"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"The market for gifting candles"},{"t":"p","x":"Over the past three years the number of people buying candles as gifts has risen steadily. Glow Candle Co. makes a range of hand-poured soy candles in recyclable glass jars with paper labels and offers a personalised label service at a premium price. Sales are almost entirely through its own website and the owners ship direct to customers by post. Customers can choose from six scents and four limited-edition seasonal scents."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing sales"},{"t":"p","x":"Over the past 12 months the owners reported a fall in sales of their core range, despite a small increase in sales of personalised candles. They are worried that they are not reaching enough new customers and want to boost sales over the next five months."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Selling at Christmas markets and pop-up stands"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Selling through gift shops and retailers."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Glow Candle Co. have allocated a budget of £40 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 20 weeks starting in mid-September."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R2","unit":2,"key":"9902","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Brew Haven — coffee subscriptions","theme":"Subscription growth campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Brew Haven.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Brew Haven.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Brew Haven.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Brew Haven. The business roasts speciality coffee and sells it by subscription. The owners want to increase the number of subscribers."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for coffee subscriptions in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK coffee market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK coffee market is worth about £4.7 billion a year. Home coffee consumption grew rapidly after 2020 and in 2024 an estimated 55% of adults drink coffee at home at least once a day. Speciality coffee – single-origin, freshly roasted beans – is the fastest growing part of the market, growing by about 9% a year compared with 2% for instant coffee. Subscription services allow customers to receive fresh beans on a regular schedule. A report by the Specialty Coffee Association found that 1 in 7 UK coffee drinkers has tried a coffee subscription, but that 38% cancel within six months, mainly because of cost or because they have too much coffee."},{"t":"p","x":"Environmental concerns are shaping the market: 64% of coffee buyers say they check whether coffee is ethically sourced, and compostable packaging is increasingly expected. Rising green-coffee prices have forced many roasters to increase prices by 10–15%. Office coffee is also changing: following the growth of hybrid working, many businesses have replaced large catering contracts with smaller subscription deals for shared kitchens."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Offer","Price (250 g bag)","Notes"],"rows":[["Bean Street","National subscription brand; app, personalised taste quiz","£9.50","120,000 subscribers; heavy online advertising"],["Monarch Roasters","Premium single-origin; coffee-tasting events","£14.00","Sponsors running and cycling events"],["Supermarket own brand","Whole-bean ranges","£4.50","Wide distribution; promotions"],["Brew Haven","Small-batch roasted, compostable packaging, 3 delivery options","£10.50","6,200 subscribers; Instagram 7,800 followers"]]},{"t":"h","x":"Brew Haven"},{"t":"p","x":"Brew Haven was started six years ago by Idris and Maya, who roast beans in an industrial unit and deliver by post. The business has 6,200 active subscribers who pay by direct debit. Over the past year the number of new subscribers has fallen by 11% and cancellations have risen. The owners have a database of 14,000 past and current customers who receive a monthly newsletter, and they have started selling a small range of brewing equipment and gift boxes. The business uses 100% compostable packaging and donates 10p from each bag to a coffee-growing community project."},{"t":"h","x":"Brew Haven: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: October 2024 · Sample size: 800 · Location: two railway stations and a university campus · Sample: individuals aged 18 to 70"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–70"],"v":["212","241","148","117","82"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How often do you drink coffee at home?","c":["Daily","A few times a week","Occasionally","Never"],"v":["418","236","104","42"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you ever had a coffee subscription?","c":["Yes – currently","Yes – cancelled","No"],"v":["96","144","560"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Why did you cancel (or why might you)? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Too expensive","Too much coffee","Lack of choice","Poor delivery","Quality"],"v":["187","231","90","76","58"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What would encourage you to subscribe? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Free first bag","Flexible delivery dates","Ethical sourcing","Discount for friends","Free equipment"],"v":["502","478","346","329","251"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a 250 g bag?","c":["Under £7","£7–£10","£10–£13","£13–£16","Over £16"],"v":["128","301","247","94","30"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where would you be happy to try coffee samples? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Online/free sample in post","Food festivals","Workplace","Sports events","Supermarkets"],"v":["455","336","268","142","203"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Instagram / Facebook ads","£0.45–£1.10 per click; targeted by age, interests and location"],["YouTube/podcast sponsorship","£300–£2,500 per episode or video depending on audience"],["Influencers","£40–£600 per post; food and lifestyle accounts; many accept free product"],["Food festivals and stalls","£120–£1,500 per weekend stall; staff £12 per hour; sampling costs about £0.35 per cup"],["Workplace/co-working partnerships","Free sample kit £6 per workplace; corporate account manager £28,000 salary (part-time £14,000); brewers loaned £90 each"],["Sponsorship (events/clubs)","£300–£3,000 per event; community running and cycling events attract 500–3,000 participants"],["Printing and packaging inserts","Leaflets £32 per 1,000; postcard inserts £18 per 1,000; branded cups £0.12 each"],["Other","Video production £1,200–£6,000 · photography £450 · voice-over £600 · website changes £2,500 · market-stand insurance £140"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"The coffee subscription market"},{"t":"p","x":"Brew Haven operates online and by post. Customers select the size and frequency of their subscription and pay monthly. The owners want to grow subscriber numbers as quickly as possible and to reduce cancellations. They have identified that many customers try subscriptions through a free first bag or at events."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing subscribers"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Brew Haven are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Partnering with workplaces and co-working spaces to offer office coffee subscriptions"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Sponsoring community sports events and selling at food festivals."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Brew Haven have allocated a budget of £55 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for six months."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R3","unit":2,"key":"9903","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Fernlea Skincare — natural skincare","theme":"Gifting and winter-skin campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Fernlea Skincare.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Fernlea Skincare.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Fernlea Skincare.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Fernlea Skincare. The business makes small-batch natural moisturisers, balms and body oils and sells them online and through a few independent shops. The owners want to increase sales over the winter gifting period."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for natural skincare in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK natural skincare market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK natural and organic beauty market is worth about £0.7 billion and has grown by around 9% a year. Buyers are mainly women aged 25 to 54, but sales to men have doubled in three years. 58% of skincare buyers say they read ingredient lists and one in three has switched brand because of environmental concerns. Gift sets are the fastest-growing format, with the biggest peaks before Mother’s Day and Christmas."},{"t":"p","x":"However, prices of natural oils and glass packaging have risen by 20% in two years and “greenwashing” scandals have reduced trust in some brands. The Advertising Standards Authority has warned small brands about unproven “natural” claims. Large retailers have launched cheaper own-label “clean beauty” ranges and now stock more than 200 natural brands online."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["Aveline","Premium, widely available; strong influencer following","£32 (50 ml cream)","Influencers, magazines, own stores"],["GreenGlow","Mid-price, sold in chemists and supermarkets","£14","TV, supermarket promotions"],["Meadow & Moss","Independent, similar size to Fernlea; strong on gift sets","£22","Instagram, craft fairs, subscription box"],["Pure Basics","Budget refillable range","£8","Price promotions, online ads"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Aveline holds an estimated 15% share of UK online natural skincare sales. Meadow & Moss increased its sales by 40% last winter after launching “build-your-own” gift boxes with a personalised message."},{"t":"h","x":"Fernlea Skincare"},{"t":"p","x":"Fernlea Skincare was started five years ago by Ruth and Callum Bevan in their kitchen and now employs three part-time staff. Products are made in small batches in a rented unit. About 80% of sales are through its website; 12 independent shops stock the range. Online sales of its core moisturisers fell 7% last year, although gift sets sold out twice. The owners believe many customers do not know about the brand’s ethical sourcing and refillable tins."},{"t":"h","x":"Fernlea Skincare: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: October 2024 · Sample size: 640 · Location: a shopping centre and two farmers’ markets · Sample: people aged 18 to 70"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–70"],"v":["62","133","143","139","104","59"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy natural skincare at least twice a year?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["406","234"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy skincare as gifts?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["322","318"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What matters most when buying skincare? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Ingredients","Price","Skin results","Eco-friendly packaging","Brand","Cruelty-free"],"v":["460","356","394","261","139","293"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a 50 ml moisturiser?","c":["Under £10","£10–£15","£15–£20","£20–£25","Over £25"],"v":["113","222","172","98","35"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where would you buy a skincare gift? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Online","Chemist","Department store","Independent shop","Market stall"],"v":["275","322","191","188","113"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which platforms do you use daily? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Instagram","TikTok","Facebook","YouTube","None"],"v":["286","197","228","187","62"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Fernlea Skincare has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Instagram and Facebook ads","£0.50–£1.20 per click; targeting by age and interests"],["Influencers","£50–£800 per post; skincare micro-influencers (10–50k followers) often accept products"],["Magazine advertorials","£1,200–£4,500 per half-page; readership mainly women 35+"],["Email marketing","£30 a month for 5,000 contacts; Fernlea has 2,800 subscribers"],["Pop-up stand in shopping centre","£600–£1,500 per week plus staffing"],["Local radio","£40 per 30-second slot; reaches 180,000 listeners"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"The gifting market"},{"t":"p","x":"Fernlea sells its products in recyclable tins and glass bottles with plastic-free labels. Gift sets and personalised messages are popular but the owners have not advertised them widely. Customers value the “made by hand” story, but the owners are concerned that their prices are higher than supermarket brands."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing winter sales"},{"t":"p","x":"Sales of the core range fell last year while gift sets sold out, and the owners want to reach many more new customers before Christmas."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Fernlea Skincare are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Building a gift-set campaign through social media, influencers and email"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Taking a pop-up stand in shopping centres and selling through gift shops and department-store concessions."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Fernlea Skincare have allocated a budget of £30 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 16 weeks starting in early October."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R4","unit":2,"key":"9904","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Cog & Chain Cycle Hire — bicycle hire","theme":"Summer season campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Cog & Chain Cycle Hire.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Cog & Chain Cycle Hire.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Cog & Chain Cycle Hire.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Cog & Chain Cycle Hire. The business rents leisure bikes and e-bikes to tourists and local families from a shop beside a popular cycle trail. The owners want to increase summer bookings, especially mid-week."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for cycle hire and tourism in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK cycle hire and tourism market"},{"t":"p","x":"UK cycling tourism is worth about £5 billion a year. E-bike hire is growing by 30% a year because it widens the market to older and less fit riders. Around 1 in 4 UK adults say they plan a cycling day out this year. Domestic holidays have grown since 2020 and more visitors book activities online, often the evening before."},{"t":"p","x":"Hire businesses are very weather-dependent: a wet July can cut income by 40%. Insurance and bike maintenance costs have risen. Councils are expanding cycle lanes but also introducing pitch charges for trail-side operators. Many visitors compare options on their phones and choose using reviews."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["TrailRiders","Largest local operator; e-bikes and guided tours","£28 per day (standard) / £55 (e-bike)","Google ads, hotel partnerships"],["Pedal Pals","Family hire and children’s bikes","£18 / £45","Leaflets in cafés and campsites"],["Hub Bikes (national)","App-based hire stations","£6 per hour","App promotions, social media"]]},{"t":"p","x":"TrailRiders is estimated to hold 35% of local hire business. Hub Bikes’ app has been downloaded by 90,000 local visitors, but riders complain it is difficult to find bikes in summer."},{"t":"h","x":"Cog & Chain Cycle Hire"},{"t":"p","x":"Cog & Chain was set up four years ago by Ellis and Kasia Marsh. The shop has 60 bikes, 15 of them e-bikes, and employs four seasonal staff. Most customers walk in. Weekend demand is high but mid-week bookings are 55% below capacity. The business has a website but only 9% of bookings are made online. Reviews are very good (4.8 stars) but only 140 customers have left one."},{"t":"h","x":"Cog & Chain Cycle Hire: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: June 2024 · Sample size: 720 · Location: the cycle trail car park, a campsite and two village centres · Sample: visitors and residents aged 16 to 75"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["16–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–75"],"v":["56","96","179","154","146","89"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you hired a bike in the last two years?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["308","412"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Who do you usually ride with?","c":["Alone","Partner/friends","Young children","Extended family","Club"],"v":["62","272","214","118","54"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How do you usually choose a hire company? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Reviews","Price","Location","Availability of e-bikes","Recommendation","Online booking"],"v":["467","381","340","277","286","246"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a day’s hire of a standard bike?","c":["Under £15","£15–£20","£20–£25","£25–£30","Over £30"],"v":["105","244","221","113","37"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where do you look for activities when on holiday? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Google","Social media","Accommodation","Tourist information","Leaflets","Friends"],"v":["415","256","328","198","155","233"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which days do you prefer to ride?","c":["Weekdays","Weekends","No preference"],"v":["213","331","176"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Cog & Chain Cycle Hire has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Google search and map ads","£0.60–£1.40 per click; bookers search “bike hire near me”"],["Facebook/Instagram ads","£0.40–£0.90 per click; family and age targeting"],["Accommodation partnerships (hotels, campsites)","10% commission on bookings; leaflets and QR codes"],["Local radio and tourist magazines","£45 per 30-second slot; £250–£600 per advert"],["Review-gathering campaign (QR codes, small incentive)","Staff time plus £1 per voucher"],["Roadside banners and trail signage","£800 per sign per season, council permission needed"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Cycle hire in the summer"},{"t":"p","x":"Cog & Chain offers hourly, half-day and full-day hire, with discounts for groups. E-bikes earn three times more per day than standard bikes but cost more to maintain. The shop opens seven days a week from Easter to October."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing bookings"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to fill the shop’s quiet mid-week slots and increase online bookings, while protecting the shop’s friendly reputation."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Cog & Chain Cycle Hire are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Offering mid-week family and group packages promoted through accommodation partners and online booking"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Running a high-profile summer events campaign (guided e-bike tours, trail festival day) promoted on social media and in local media."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Cog & Chain Cycle Hire have allocated a budget of £35 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for five months from April."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R5","unit":2,"key":"9905","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Little Sprout Toys — wooden toys","theme":"Christmas toy campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Little Sprout Toys.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Little Sprout Toys.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Little Sprout Toys.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Little Sprout Toys. The business designs and sells wooden and plastic-free toys for children under 8 through its website and market stalls. The owners want to increase sales in the run-up to Christmas."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for children’s toys in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK children’s toys market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK toy market is worth about £3.5 billion; around 1 in 3 toys are sold in the six weeks before Christmas. “Eco” and “heirloom” toys are growing faster than the market as parents seek plastic-free gifts. Grandparents and relatives buy 40% of children’s gifts. Online accounts for 55% of toy sales, and videos on social media strongly influence children’s requests."},{"t":"p","x":"Large retailers dominate, with heavy discounting in November. Toy safety regulations require testing, which adds cost. Rising wood prices and shipping costs have squeezed small makers’ margins. Many parents say they feel pressure on budgets: average spend per child at Christmas has fallen 6% this year."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["Toybox Warehouse","Big-box retailer; huge range; heavy discounts","£12 (typical toy)","TV, catalogue, price matching"],["Woodland Wonders","Premium wooden toys; sold in gift shops","£34","Magazines, Instagram, trade shows"],["PlayGreen","Mid-price eco toys; recycled plastics","£19","Online ads, influencers (parents)"],["Marketplace sellers","Thousands of small makers on online platforms","£10–£40","Platform search ads"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Woodland Wonders has an estimated 8% share of the UK eco-toy market and ran a successful “toy library” promotion last year. PlayGreen’s parent influencers generated 1.2 million video views."},{"t":"h","x":"Little Sprout Toys"},{"t":"p","x":"Little Sprout Toys was founded by Sam and Joy Adeyemi, a teacher and a carpenter. They make toys from sustainably sourced wood in a workshop at home, and employ two packers at peak times. Customers praise the quality but the business has only 1,900 followers on social media. Sales of ride-on toys fell 15% last year while puzzles and play kitchens sold well. Prices are 25% above the market average."},{"t":"h","x":"Little Sprout Toys: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: September 2024 · Sample size: 560 · Location: a children’s play centre, a supermarket car park and a family event · Sample: adults aged 20 to 70, mainly parents and grandparents"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["20–29","30–39","40–49","50–59","60–70"],"v":["91","195","117","87","70"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy toys at least four times a year?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["323","237"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you buy toys for a child that is not your own?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["342","218"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What is most important when buying a toy? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Safety","Quality/durability","Child’s wishes","Price","Eco-friendly materials","Educational value"],"v":["377","290","304","272","220","250"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a good quality wooden toy?","c":["Under £10","£10–£20","£20–£30","£30–£40","Over £40"],"v":["63","184","174","99","40"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where would you buy a Christmas toy? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Online","Large toy store","Supermarket","Independent shop","Christmas market"],"v":["343","215","224","150","125"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which platforms do you use daily? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Facebook","Instagram","TikTok","YouTube","None"],"v":["283","232","150","206","52"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Little Sprout Toys has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Facebook ads aimed at parents and grandparents","£0.35–£0.80 per click; 38 million UK users"],["Parent influencers","£40–£500 per video; gift guide features are popular in November"],["Christmas markets and fairs","£150–£600 per weekend stall"],["Gift-guide listings in magazines/websites","£300–£2,000 for inclusion"],["School newsletters and parent groups","Low cost; £50–£150 for sponsored posts"],["Email and gift-card reminders","£25 a month for 3,000 contacts"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"The Christmas toy market"},{"t":"p","x":"Little Sprout Toys sells about 60% of its annual revenue between October and December. The owners offer free gift wrapping and personalised name carvings. Packing capacity limits the business to around 150 orders per day."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing Christmas sales"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to win new customers before Christmas without damaging the brand’s premium, eco-friendly image or exceeding packing capacity."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Little Sprout Toys are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Running an online campaign targeting parents and grandparents through social media, influencers and gift guides"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Selling more in person through Christmas markets, school fairs and a seasonal pop-up shop."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Little Sprout Toys have allocated a budget of £28 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 12 weeks from mid-September."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R6","unit":2,"key":"9906","series":"RA10 practice","title":"FuelBox Meals — healthy meal delivery","theme":"New customer acquisition campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for FuelBox Meals.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for FuelBox Meals.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of FuelBox Meals.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called FuelBox Meals. The business prepares healthy ready-made meals for fitness-minded customers and delivers them weekly. The owners want to increase the number of regular customers over the next six months."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for meal-prep and healthy food delivery in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK meal-prep and healthy food delivery market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK meal-kit and ready-meal delivery market is worth about £2.8 billion. Healthy and high-protein meals are growing by 15% a year, driven by gym members and people working from home. Around 22% of adults aged 18–34 have tried a food-delivery subscription. Customers expect flexible plans, with skip-and-pause options, and clear nutritional information."},{"t":"p","x":"The cost of living has led many subscribers to cancel: average subscription churn is 8% a month. Delivery costs have risen with fuel and packaging prices. Supermarkets have launched “healthy meal for two” ranges at around £4 per portion, and food-delivery apps now partner with restaurants offering high-protein menus."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["GymFuel (national)","Largest fitness meal brand; endorsed by athletes","£6.50 per meal","Sports sponsorship, influencers, TV"],["FreshPlate","Premium chef-designed meals","£9.00","Instagram, magazine features"],["Supermarket healthy ranges","Convenient, in-store","£4.00","In-store promotions, loyalty cards"],["Local rival: MacroKitchen","Smaller regional business, similar offer","£7.00","Gym partnerships, Facebook"]]},{"t":"p","x":"GymFuel holds about 28% of the fitness-meal market. MacroKitchen has agreements with 12 gyms where members receive a 15% discount."},{"t":"h","x":"FuelBox Meals"},{"t":"p","x":"FuelBox Meals was started by personal trainer Dean Hughes and chef Aisha Rahman. Meals are cooked in a rented commercial kitchen and delivered by van within a 40-mile radius twice a week. The business has 420 regular customers, but 3 in 10 cancel within three months. Customers praise taste and portion size but say menus can be repetitive. FuelBox has 5,300 social-media followers and spent very little on marketing last year."},{"t":"h","x":"FuelBox Meals: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: November 2024 · Sample size: 680 · Location: three gyms, a business park and an online panel · Sample: adults aged 18 to 60"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–60"],"v":["110","236","169","102","63"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you go to the gym or exercise at least twice a week?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["466","214"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you ever used a meal delivery service?","c":["Yes – currently","Yes – cancelled","No"],"v":["93","127","460"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What would encourage you to subscribe? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Free first week","Flexible plans","Variety of menus","Nutritional information","Discounts through my gym","Vegan/vegetarian choice"],"v":["391","406","396","302","273","212"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a meal?","c":["Under £5","£5–£6.50","£6.50–£8","£8–£9.50","Over £9.50"],"v":["135","233","181","92","39"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where do you hear about food brands? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Instagram","TikTok","Friends","Gym or club","YouTube","Search engines"],"v":["320","233","285","253","164","190"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Why might you cancel a subscription? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Cost","Repetitive menu","Poor delivery","Taste","Change in routine"],"v":["419","289","151","178","234"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"FuelBox Meals has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Instagram/TikTok ads","£0.45–£1.10 per click; fitness interests and age targeting"],["Fitness influencers (micro)","£60–£500 per post; discount-code tracking"],["Gym partnerships","Sponsor a gym for £1,500 a year; member discounts and sampling"],["Google search ads","£0.70–£1.60 per click"],["Workplace sampling","Staff time + ingredients (about £400 per event)"],["Referral scheme","Free meal for each new customer referred, approx. £6.50 cost each"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Meal delivery in the UK"},{"t":"p","x":"FuelBox Meals offers three plans (10, 14 or 20 meals a week), with six new meals added each month. The brand aims to be a trusted, friendly alternative to larger national suppliers. The kitchen can make 4,000 meals a week."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing regular customers"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to reduce cancellations and win new regular customers, but they are worried about overstretching their small kitchen and driver team."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of FuelBox Meals are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Targeting gym members through gym partnerships, sampling and discounts"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Building the brand online through social media, influencers and a referral scheme for existing customers."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of FuelBox Meals have allocated a budget of £45 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for six months."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R7","unit":2,"key":"9907","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Pawfect Treats — dog treats","theme":"Subscription box growth campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Pawfect Treats.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Pawfect Treats.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Pawfect Treats.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Pawfect Treats. The business bakes natural dog treats and sells a monthly subscription box as well as single bags online. The owners want to increase the number of subscription boxes sold."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for pet food and treats in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK pet food and treats market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK pet care market is worth about £8 billion. Around 13 million households own a pet and 60% of dog owners say they “treat their dog like a family member”. The natural and premium pet-food segment has grown by 12% a year. Owners aged 25 to 44 are most likely to subscribe to pet products online."},{"t":"p","x":"Rising food costs and cost-of-living pressure have led some owners to trade down to supermarket brands, and subscription cancellations have increased. Pet-product claims are regulated (for example, health claims need evidence). Dog-owner communities on social media strongly influence buying: 4 in 10 owners say they have bought a product after seeing it recommended by another owner."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["Barkbox Monthly","National subscription box; toys and treats","£22 per box","Influencers, YouTube, TV"],["NaturalPaw","Premium natural treats in pet shops","£6 per 150 g bag","Pet-shop displays, vets’ recommendations"],["Supermarket treats","Mass-market, low price","£2.50","In-store promotions"],["Local bakeries and market stalls","Handmade treats sold at fairs","£4–£7","Word of mouth, markets"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Barkbox has an estimated 40% share of UK pet subscription boxes. NaturalPaw gained 150 new shops last year through vets’ endorsements."},{"t":"h","x":"Pawfect Treats"},{"t":"p","x":"Pawfect Treats was set up three years ago by Gemma and Tariq Hussain. Treats are baked in a small unit using UK-sourced ingredients with no artificial additives. The business has 780 box subscribers and sells bags online to 3,100 past customers. Most subscribers join after seeing a dog-owner friend’s photo on social media. Subscription churn is 9% a month, and the owners have little time for marketing. They would like to expand but are worried about demand fluctuations and ingredient prices."},{"t":"h","x":"Pawfect Treats: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: August 2024 · Sample size: 600 · Location: dog parks, a pet shop and a country show · Sample: dog owners aged 18 to 70"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–70"],"v":["50","149","143","116","93","49"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How many dogs do you own?","c":["One","Two","Three or more"],"v":["393","164","43"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you bought treats online in the last six months?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["269","331"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What matters most when buying treats? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Natural ingredients","Price","Dog’s enjoyment","Health benefits","UK-made","Brand reputation"],"v":["407","300","429","274","191","142"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a 150 g bag?","c":["Under £3","£3–£4.50","£4.50–£6","£6–£8","Over £8"],"v":["84","183","191","108","34"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Where do you find new products for your dog? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Instagram","Facebook groups","Vets","Pet shop","Friends","Dog events"],"v":["208","180","166","272","307","124"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Would you consider a monthly treat box?","c":["Yes","Maybe","No"],"v":["144","215","241"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Pawfect Treats has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Facebook and Instagram ads","£0.30–£0.80 per click; dog-owner interests"],["Dog-owner micro-influencers","£25–£300 per post; many accept treats for coverage"],["Dog shows and country fairs","£120–£500 per day for a stall; free samples"],["Vet and groomer partnerships","10% commission; leaflets and sample bags"],["Email and loyalty rewards","£20 a month for up to 4,000 contacts"],["Local newspaper advert","£200–£450 per quarter-page"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Dog treats and subscriptions"},{"t":"p","x":"Pawfect Treats offers a standard box (£18) and a large box (£26) with free delivery. Subscribers can pause or cancel at any time. The owners can bake enough for 1,500 boxes a month."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing subscribers"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to double the number of subscription boxes within six months, but must keep churn low and protect their natural, UK-made reputation."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Pawfect Treats are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Building an online community of dog owners through social media, micro-influencers and a referral scheme"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Reaching owners in person through dog shows, vets, groomers and pet shops with samples and discount cards."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Pawfect Treats have allocated a budget of £32 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for six months starting in January."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R8","unit":2,"key":"9908","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Verde Plant Studio — houseplants and workshops","theme":"Spring growth campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Verde Plant Studio.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Verde Plant Studio.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Verde Plant Studio.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Verde Plant Studio. The business sells houseplants online and runs plant-care workshops in a small studio. The owners want to increase sales and workshop bookings in spring."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for houseplants and indoor gardening in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK houseplants and indoor gardening market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK houseplant market is worth about £0.5 billion and has grown by 8% a year. Around 70% of 18–34-year-olds own at least one houseplant and “plant parents” are a strong social-media community. Online sales have grown by 20% a year, but plants are fragile and returns are costly. Workshops such as terrariums and kokedama are popular gifts and social experiences."},{"t":"p","x":"Garden centres and supermarkets sell cheaper plants, often at £4–£8. Plant prices have risen because of energy costs for heated greenhouses and transport. Customers compare prices easily online, and negative reviews about damaged plants spread quickly. Trends change fast: a plant that is popular on social media can sell out within days."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["Leaf & Co (national online)","Wide range; plants delivered in branded boxes","£18–£45","Influencers, TV, discount codes"],["Garden centre chain","Large range; in-store advice","£8–£25","Seasonal promotions, loyalty card"],["Supermarkets","Cheap, limited range","£4–£8","In-store displays"],["Studio Fern","Local workshops and plants","£35 per workshop","Instagram, local events"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Leaf & Co has an estimated 12% share of UK online plant sales; Studio Fern sells out its workshops two weeks ahead."},{"t":"h","x":"Verde Plant Studio"},{"t":"p","x":"Verde Plant Studio was founded by Marta Nowak, a former florist, with a studio and polytunnel behind her house. The business sells about 300 plants a month online and runs 12 workshops a month. Customers love Marta’s care guides but delivery damage affects 6% of orders. Workshop bookings are strong in winter but drop in spring, when customers are outdoors. The business has 4,600 followers and an email list of 1,900."},{"t":"h","x":"Verde Plant Studio: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: February 2025 · Sample size: 700 · Location: a garden centre, a shopping centre and an online panel · Sample: adults aged 18 to 65"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–65"],"v":["105","209","169","126","91"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Do you own at least one houseplant?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["521","179"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you bought plants online?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["263","437"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What matters most when buying plants? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Healthy plant","Price","Care advice","Rare varieties","Delivery quality","Pot design"],"v":["519","410","370","201","375","237"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a medium-sized houseplant?","c":["Under £8","£8–£14","£14–£20","£20–£30","Over £30"],"v":["127","218","207","107","41"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Would you attend a plant workshop?","c":["Yes","Maybe","No"],"v":["155","263","282"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which platforms do you use daily? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Instagram","TikTok","Pinterest","Facebook","YouTube","None"],"v":["361","241","127","228","190","56"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Verde Plant Studio has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Instagram/Pinterest ads","£0.40–£1.00 per click; interest targeting"],["Plant influencers and YouTube creators","£50–£600 per video; care videos work well"],["Workshop gift vouchers","Printing and promotion about £300; seasonal peaks (Mother’s Day)"],["Local community events and markets","£80–£300 per stall"],["Email newsletter and care guides","£25 a month; 1,900 subscribers"],["Local press and community radio","£150–£400 per feature"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Plants and workshops"},{"t":"p","x":"Verde Plant Studio offers 120 plant varieties, workshop vouchers and a “plant-care club” for £6 a month. Plants are packed in recyclable boxes but the owners are testing stronger packaging. The studio can host 20 people at a time."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing spring sales"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to keep workshops full during spring and increase online plant sales without increasing damaged deliveries."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Verde Plant Studio are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Growing the online business through social media, influencers and email marketing"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Promoting workshops and vouchers through local events, community groups and press to build a local customer base."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Verde Plant Studio have allocated a budget of £24 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 16 weeks from March."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R9","unit":2,"key":"9909","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Northstar Language Academy — adult language classes","theme":"Autumn enrolment campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Northstar Language Academy.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Northstar Language Academy.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Northstar Language Academy.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Northstar Language Academy. The business teaches evening and weekend language classes in a rented training centre and online. The owners want to increase enrolments for the autumn term."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for adult language learning in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK adult language learning market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK adult education and language-learning market is worth about £1.1 billion. Spanish, French and Italian are the most popular languages, and demand for Japanese and Korean has doubled in five years. Around 1 in 6 adults say they would like to learn a language in the next year. Free apps have raised awareness but many users stop within weeks: 70% of people who start an app drop out in a month."},{"t":"p","x":"Local authority funding for adult education has fallen, so some council classes have closed. Learners increasingly want flexible online options and short courses linked to travel or careers. Classroom costs are high; tutor pay and venue hire have increased by 10% in two years. Reviews and trial lessons strongly influence enrolment decisions."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["LinguaApp (national)","Subscription app; gamified lessons","£9 per month","TV, social media, free trials"],["City College evening classes","Funded courses; recognised qualifications","£160 per 10-week term","Prospectus, website, open evenings"],["TutorMatch","Online one-to-one tutors","£25 per hour","Search ads, tutor profiles"],["Local language café","Informal conversation groups","£5 per session","Facebook, flyers in cafés"]]},{"t":"p","x":"LinguaApp is the market leader with an estimated 30% of users but low completion rates. City College fills 85% of its places; Northstar fills 58%."},{"t":"h","x":"Northstar Language Academy"},{"t":"p","x":"Northstar Language Academy was founded by Isabel Moreno and Jun Takeda, both experienced teachers. It runs 28 classes a week in six languages, with small groups of up to 10 learners. Student satisfaction is 94%, but most students find the academy through friends. The website has not been updated for two years, and online classes account for only 10% of income. Autumn is the peak enrolment period but numbers were 15% lower than the year before."},{"t":"h","x":"Northstar Language Academy: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: June 2024 · Sample size: 760 · Location: a college campus, a library and an online survey · Sample: adults aged 18 to 75"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["18–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–75"],"v":["83","157","155","135","135","95"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you studied a language in the last five years?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["278","482"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Would you like to learn a language in the next year?","c":["Yes","Maybe","No"],"v":["224","218","318"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What would motivate you to join a class? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Travel","Career","Family/heritage","Enjoyment","Meeting people","Qualification"],"v":["396","266","180","374","300","168"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a 10-week course?","c":["Under £80","£80–£120","£120–£160","£160–£200","Over £200"],"v":["169","235","204","114","38"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How would you prefer to learn?","c":["In person","Online live","Online recorded","Mixture"],"v":["269","182","94","215"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which platforms or sources influence you? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Facebook","Instagram","YouTube","Google reviews","Friends","Local press"],"v":["281","241","266","429","397","136"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Northstar Language Academy has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Google search and review ads","£0.80–£2.00 per click; strong intent"],["Facebook/Instagram ads","£0.40–£1.00 per click; age and interest targeting"],["Trial lesson events (free taster evenings)","Venue and tutor time about £250 each"],["Local press and library noticeboards","£100–£400 per advert"],["Partnerships with employers","Corporate discount packages; staff time"],["Referral discounts","£20 off for each friend referred"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Language classes"},{"t":"p","x":"Northstar offers classes for beginners to advanced learners. It uses a communicative method with small groups and homework through a basic learner portal. The centre has capacity for 14 classes in the evenings and weekends and has space for another 120 learners."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing enrolments"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to fill empty places for the autumn term and increase online enrolments, while keeping their reputation for friendly, high-quality teaching."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Northstar Language Academy are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Promoting taster evenings and referral discounts through local media and community partners"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Building online enrolments through search and social media advertising and a refreshed website."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Northstar Language Academy have allocated a budget of £26 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for 14 weeks from late June."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180},{"id":"U2-R10","unit":2,"key":"9910","series":"RA10 practice","title":"Tidemark Surf School — surf lessons","theme":"Summer lessons campaign","source":"ra10","kind":"set","total":70,"acts":[{"n":1,"marks":34,"text":"Prepare a rationale for a marketing campaign for Tidemark Surf School.\n• marketing aims and objectives\n• analysis of research data on the market and competition\n• an evaluation of the reliability and validity of the information provided\n• a justification for your rationale.","kind":"rationale"},{"n":2,"marks":36,"text":"Based on your rationale from Activity 1, you must produce a plan for the marketing campaign for Tidemark Surf School.\n• marketing mix\n• marketing message\n• selection of media\n• campaign budget\n• timescale.\nYou must produce this plan in an appropriate written format for the owners of Tidemark Surf School.","kind":"plan"}],"partA":[{"t":"h","x":"Set Task Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"You have been asked to write a proposal for a marketing campaign for a business called Tidemark Surf School. The business runs surf lessons and board hire from a beach hut on the north Cornwall coast. The owners want to increase bookings between May and September."},{"t":"p","x":"You are required to use the research you have been given to independently analyse the market for outdoor activity and surf tourism in the UK. This research includes primary and secondary data relating to: competitors; market size, share and structure; target market; trends; external influences; and media options. You are allowed a maximum of two sides of A4 of your individually prepared notes and you may annotate Part A to support you during the Part B supervised assessment."},{"t":"h","x":"The UK outdoor activity and surf tourism market"},{"t":"p","x":"The UK adventure tourism market is estimated at £3.4 billion, with water sports growing by 12% a year. Surfing is now among the top five activities visitors try on UK beach holidays. Most bookings are made within a week of arrival and 70% of customers read reviews before booking. Families with children aged 8 to 16 are the biggest segment."},{"t":"p","x":"The business is seasonal and weather-dependent: poor surf or storms lead to cancellations. Insurance, wetsuit replacement and beach-access fees have risen. Many new schools have opened in the last three years and several accommodation providers now bundle activities with stays. Concerns about water quality and safety strongly influence parents’ choices."},{"t":"h","x":"Competitors"},{"t":"grid","head":["Business","Position in the market","Typical price","Main promotion"],"rows":[["WaveRiders","Largest school on the coast; accredited instructors","£45 per 2-hour group lesson","Hotel partnerships, Google ads"],["Blue Break","Premium small-group and private lessons","£70","Instagram, magazine features"],["Beach Hut Hire","Board and wetsuit hire only","£15 per day","Roadside signs, leaflets"],["Camp & Surf","Campsite with lessons included in stay","£35 (bundled)","Campsite website, social media"]]},{"t":"p","x":"WaveRiders has an estimated 30% share of local lessons; Camp & Surf has grown quickly by bundling lessons with accommodation."},{"t":"h","x":"Tidemark Surf School"},{"t":"p","x":"Tidemark Surf School was founded six years ago by Jory and Beth Pascoe. It employs six qualified instructors during the season and has 45 boards and 80 wetsuits. Most customers are walk-ups or come from referrals, and only 20% of bookings are online. Weekends in July and August are fully booked but mid-week and early/late season bookings are weak. Customer satisfaction is high; 96% say they would recommend the school."},{"t":"h","x":"Tidemark Surf School: Market Research Report"},{"t":"p","x":"Month: May 2024 · Sample size: 620 · Location: beach car parks, a holiday park and a surf shop · Sample: visitors and locals aged 8 to 65 (with parents for under-16s)"},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Age","c":["8–15","16–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–65"],"v":["77","96","140","152","97","58"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Have you tried surfing before?","c":["Yes","No"],"v":["188","432"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"When do you book activities on holiday?","c":["Before travelling","During first days","On the day"],"v":["217","279","124"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"What is most important when choosing a school? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Safety/qualifications","Price","Reviews","Instructor friendliness","Location","Equipment included","Group size"],"v":["465","307","351","257","243","229","292"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How much would you pay for a 2-hour group lesson?","c":["Under £30","£30–£40","£40–£50","£50–£60","Over £60"],"v":["76","180","204","123","37"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"How do you find activities? (Select all that apply.)","c":["Google","Accommodation","Social media","Friends","Tourist information","Signs on the beach"],"v":["335","261","227","277","122","169"]}},{"t":"stat","x":{"q":"Which time would you most like to surf?","c":["Early morning","Mid-morning","Afternoon","Evening"],"v":["81","246","238","55"]}},{"t":"h","x":"Research on media selection"},{"t":"p","x":"Tidemark Surf School has researched the following media options for its campaign."},{"t":"grid","head":["Media option","Costs and information"],"rows":[["Google and review-site ads","£0.70–£1.80 per click; strong booking intent"],["Instagram/TikTok ads","£0.40–£1.00 per click; videos of lessons perform well"],["Accommodation and holiday-park partnerships","10–15% commission; leaflets and welcome packs"],["Local radio and tourist guides","£300–£900 per advertising package"],["School and youth-group offers","Discounted mid-week lessons; staff time to visit"],["Beach signage and banners","£600 per season plus permits"]]}],"partB":[{"t":"h","x":"Surf lessons in the summer"},{"t":"p","x":"Tidemark offers beginner lessons, improvers’ sessions and private coaching, with equipment included. The school is accredited and provides a photo package on request. Instructors are paid per session so mid-week gaps reduce staff earnings."},{"t":"h","x":"Increasing bookings"},{"t":"p","x":"The owners want to fill mid-week and shoulder-season sessions and increase online bookings, without compromising safety or small-group lessons."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Tidemark Surf School are considering two options to help them achieve their goal:"},{"t":"p","x":"EITHER"},{"t":"p","x":"Partnering with accommodation providers and running mid-week family packages"},{"t":"p","x":"OR"},{"t":"p","x":"Promoting the school online through search ads, social media video and customer reviews."},{"t":"p","x":"The owners of Tidemark Surf School have allocated a budget of £30 000 for the campaign. The campaign should run for five months from early May."}],"prep":{},"minutes":180}];
